c25b2b600b7cb2500b,2027-03-31,holding,2.19394168994151210686077801262648979,0.548485422485378026715194503156622448,-1.23257900591082760857896321403062281,0.268596450536012472266757679207286210,1.22623762298654213349781390909627153,0.0638531506849315068493150684931506850,4.04198704499948130268147021588692132,1.48694263423928688011014490765394799,1.70083259570308538970779842725137091,26.4000000000000000000000000000000000,9.4138,26.8484626109242388790635613143496756,0\\r\\n9c243124e5e4d33278929388ff72fd8e2163d783a4f6cac25b2b600b7cb2500b,2027-04-19,holding,3.61211153331185033408114013667345440,0.903027883327962583520285034168363600,-2.02002971429774554104292166413601763,0.424043256572555808304895880675188215,1.98290181933923367807730486358888456,0.101100821917808219178082191780821918,1.70083259570308538970779842725137091,-2.52121132847325895551033714737945378,-2.16906128595254116819466639064493620,26.0566735112936344969199178644763860,9.4138,26.8484626109242388790635613143496756,0\\r\\n\"","\"# Riyaasat Lifestyle Limited\\n## 1. Executive assessment\\n\\n**Hold, unresolved accounting, funding and tradability.** This is the ethnic-fashion retailer Riyaasat Lifestyle Limited, not a life insurer. The supplied label “Riyaasat Life” resolves to ISIN INE0KYI01012, BSE SME 544804, symbol RIYAASAT. No corporate name change was found: it was incorporated under its current name on 23 October 2021. The CIN prefix changed from unlisted U to listed L, not the business identity. [RHP, printed 199; SECURITY]\\n\\nThere is a genuine operating business and revenue growth, but the evidence does not establish a high-quality, self-financing compounder. Revenue rose from ₹20.9349cr in FY23 to ₹33.3746cr in FY26, a comparable three-year CAGR of **16.82%**. FY26 inventory was ₹41.0462cr, larger than annual revenue, with a physical-verification limitation and inventory maintained outside the main accounting software. Bank statements have not reconciled to books in two successive audits. Four-year disclosed cumulative CFO/PAT is **-0.359**, not an average of annual ratios. [RHP, printed 230–233; AR26_BSE, PDF60–61,69–71,96–97]\\n\\nThe listing raised ₹30.19728cr gross, not all cash still available in October. A subsequent ₹15.5cr property purchase and revised store commitments materially change the investment case. Current cash, available facilities and a reliable BSE trading history remain unverified. An apparently moderate **20.35×** current-share FY26 P/E therefore is not a margin-of-safety conclusion. No trade, position allocation or favorable investment ranking is recommended.\\n\\n**Decisive variable:** can independently verified inventory turn into cash without material markdowns while the expanded store plan is funded and generates returns above its cost of capital? The strongest competing interpretation is that inventory and owned property are investment ahead of profitable growth. That interpretation needs evidence, not an EBITDA extrapolation.\\n\\n## 2. Research header, gates and confidence\\n\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Market reference: **19 October 2026**. Later research retrieval dates do not extend the information cutoff.\\n- Scope: standalone company. FY26 directors report says no subsidiary, joint venture or associate company. Related-party “associate concerns” are not assumed to be equity-accounted investments.\\n- Accounting: Indian AS, Schedule III Division I, not IndAS. Store rent is an operating expense; do not graft lease-adjusted peer EBITDA multiples onto these accounts.\\n- Price: **₹98.4, rounded secondary 19 October close**, not a verified executable BSE price. Issued shares **10,744,478**, lot **1,200**, authorized shares **15,500,000**. The dated NSE interoperability master verifies security metadata, not an NSE listing or BSE turnover.\\n- Reference position: ₹5 lakh. Confidence: identity high; historical revenue medium-high; inventory/earnings and current funding low; execution low.\\n\\n| Gate | Assessment | Evidence and required resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Conditional | Family control, old related-party exposure and disclosure errors; no established fraud/default. Obtain corrected notes and succession/RPT schedules. |\\n| G2 Accounting | **Hold, unresolved** | FY26 inventory-verification limitation; repeated bank-versus-books mismatches; historical audit-trail weakness. Reconcile independently. |\\n| G3 Funding | **Hold, unresolved** | Very low audited cash, high debt, property and expanded store commitments; October facilities and cash unavailable. A simulation failure is not evidence of an actual default. |\\n| G4 Tradability | **Hold, unresolved** | 20/60-session BSE traded-value statistics, current spread/depth and surveillance status unavailable. Missing bars are not zero-trade observations. |\\n\\nThe audit reports’ unmodified main/IFC opinions are positive contrary evidence, not a reason to ignore their annexures. No actual G1 or G3 failure override is imposed merely because a conservative forward model fails.\\n\\n## 3. Canonical scorecard and scoring audit\\n\\n| Score | Result | Coverage | Known contribution | Uncapped bounds | Effective bounds |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|---|\\n|BQS|NS|80.00%|3.4600|3.66–5.46|3.66–5.46|\\n|GQS|NS|70.00%|4.0000|4.30–7.00|4.30–5.00|\\n|IAS|NS|0.00%|0.0000|1.00–10.00|1.00–10.00|\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Core reasoning | Contrary evidence / uncertainty |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Concentrated family control; recurring reporting/control weaknesses. | Unmodified opinions, no established fraud/default; shareholder approval for changed objects. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Local design/customization capability, but six-store scale, regional dependence and nonexclusive supply. | Premium mix and gross economics suggest a local franchise; no audited repeat-cohort advantage. |\\n| REV | 7 | 16.82% three-year nominal organic revenue CAGR; no acquired operating company identified. | Unit/price/SSSG decomposition ND; new store and short history matter. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Recurring wedding/festival use and broad men/women assortment. | Discretionary demand, fashion changes and geographic concentration. |\\n| CAP | NS | Investment base sharply expanded; property advances retained in invested capital. | New-capital cohort returns and inventory validity are insufficiently established. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Persistent weak cumulative conversion before capex; inventory consumes funds. | FY25 positive CFO, but capital-advance/tax classifications affect its quality. |\\n| BAL | NS | ₹41.36cr audited debt versus ₹0.128cr cash; major later commitments. | IPO provided capital; no default reported, but current usable liquidity ND. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Mumbai added; then IPO locations, comparable cost and deadline changed. | Approved changes may be economically sensible, but delivery/returns not yet demonstrated. |\\n| EARN | NS | Core EBITDA is reconstructed, not copied from inconsistent KPI definitions. | Inventory limitation prevents a reliable earnings-quality score. |\\n| R | NS | No verified funded base-case IRR. | Conditional simulations show requirements, not actual financing availability. |\\n| D | NS | Inventory haircut, supplier withdrawal and property illiquidity can impair equity. | Recoverable liquidation values and probabilities ND. |\\n| P | NS | Apparent P/E is measurable conditionally; current intrinsic value is not. | Accounting/funding uncertainty defeats a numeric margin-of-safety claim. |\\n| L | NS | Position/lot defined, required trading-value observations absent. | Listing and metadata do not establish executable volume. |\\n\\nThe actual v3 weights are used exactly. BQS: GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS: REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS: final unrounded BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. No missing factor is replaced with 5, and no weight is renormalized. Bound = known weighted contribution plus missing weight × [1,10]. Persistent CASH2 caps GQS at5, including its effective upper bound; it does **not** convert NS to a score. GOV3 and proven-G3-failure boundary tests are retained separately. Decimal arithmetic and half-up display rounding are used throughout. Each canonical factor’s supporting facts, contrary fact, source IDs and confidence are in `factor_evidence.json`.\\n\\nTwo uncertain scored factors are tested at ±1. GOV3/GOV5 boundary cases are retained; the severe-governance cap in the GOV3 case is hypothetical, not the current GOV4 judgment. Separately, MOAT3 lowers BQS raw bounds to 3.46–5.26, while MOAT5 raises them to 3.86–5.66. MOAT has no direct GQS weight, so GQS is unchanged. IAS remains NS in both cases; its informative nested bounds shift by −0.05 and +0.05 respectively through the 25% BQS weight. These sensitivities do not fill missing factors or establish investability.\\n\\n## 4. Business model and economic anatomy\\n\\nRiyaasat designs, manufactures through its production ecosystem, sources and retails ethnic apparel for men and women, including sherwanis, lehenga choli, Indo-western outfits, kurtas and Jodhpuris. The customer is primarily an occasionwear buyer, not a contracted industrial customer. Customization, local taste, assortment and the in-store experience matter. Merchandise ownership, physical stores and fashion obsolescence make this a cash-intensive retail model, not an asset-light brand royalty stream. [RHP, business section 151–198]\\n\\nManufacturing represented about 35.06% of FY25 revenue and trading 64.94%; the January 2026 mix moved to roughly 25.58% manufacturing. This is not pure manufacturing growth. FY25’s five leading categories represented 74.09% of sales: sherwani 19.84%, lehenga choli 19.19%, Indo-western 16.02%, kurta 12.13% and Jodhpuri6.91%. A changing mix can alter margins without demonstrating price increases. B2B selling through PSL Retail/Pernia’s Pop-Up is disclosed, but no audited channel-profit or repeat-customer cohort is available. [RHP]\\n\\nThe disclosed six stores total about 25,900sqft: four Ahmedabad locations, Vadodara and owned Santacruz Mumbai. Five stores were present in FY23–FY25; Mumbai made six in FY26. RHP milestone language and store-opening tables are not perfectly aligned on initial timing, so exact per-store cohorts are not manufactured. Gujarat accounted for 96.59% of FY25 revenue and 67.13% of the January 2026 period; Maharashtra and other channels explain much of the mix shift. No export-led scaling thesis is established.\\n\\nDirect materials/traded merchandise plus separately disclosed manufacturing expenses are combined into a broad COGS proxy. The naïve FY26 sales less material/purchases/inventory-change calculation overstates gross margin because job work, labour and consumables sit in other expenses. The broad FY26 COGS proxy is ₹10.2653cr and gross margin about 69.24%, still subject to payroll-allocation and inventory-valuation limits. Rent of ₹3.1424cr is already expensed.\\n\\n## 5. Competitive position and named economic peers\\n\\nThe local franchise is credible, but durable pricing power is not demonstrated by one year’s high accounting margin. There are no exclusive supplier arrangements, and expansion outside familiar western markets requires different demand knowledge. Disclosed supplier-concentration table headings and percentage denominators are inconsistent: the stated percentages cannot simply be treated as percentages of revenue. No precise supplier-share claim is inferred from that mislabeled denominator. [RHP, printed 24–25]\\n\\n| Peer | FY26 revenue | FY26 PAT | Raw CFO/PAT | 19 Oct price | P/E |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|Vedant Fashions Limited|1435.50|375.50|1.28|509.85|32.99|\\n|Go Fashion (India) Limited|838.01|59.18|2.78|265.40|23.59|\\n|Sai Silks (Kalamandir) Limited|1653.67|140.92|2.29|72.54|7.89|\\n\\nAll peer prices above are from the official **19 October 2026 NSE bhavcopy**, and FY26 operating facts come from primary reports, not an aggregator comparison. [PEERMARKET; VEDANT26; GO26; SAI26]\\n\\n- **Vedant Fashions:** closest weddingwear/men’s occasionwear brand comparison, but 669 EBOs and franchise/wholesale economics differ sharply. FY26 reported revenue grew 3.5%, retail customer sales 6.1%, SSSG2.7%. Raw CFO/PAT is about 128%, not the presentation’s adjusted 98% cash-conversion metric. Lease principal plus interest was ₹192.4cr and capex ₹12.3cr. Large financial assets, brand reach and different capital ownership mean its multiple is not transferable.\\n- **Go Fashion:** useful store/inventory execution comparator, not a weddingwear clone. Revenue declined, PAT fell and cash-flow optics are affected by IndAS leases. CFO₹164.7048cr less lease cash ₹139.3933cr and capex ₹39.1573cr leaves negative cash. The primary FY26 report has 52,595,984 post-buyback shares; the dated exchange security master still carries 54,008,984. Both denominator valuations are retained rather than silently adopting a stale master. Weighted EPS is not used for current market cap.\\n- **Sai Silks:** ethnic-fashion retail with 81 stores and 3% SSSG, but different regional, women’s and store-size mix. ₹322.52cr CFO benefited from a₹137.14cr payable increase. Subtract₹50.64cr capex and ₹43.90cr lease principal; finance cash ₹30.98cr is separately disclosed. Do not compare this principal-only lease deduction directly with peers’ all-in lease cash without that adjustment.\\n\\nThese primary peers show that same-store growth can be far below new-store growth, inventory ties up cash, and rent accounting matters. A simple average P/E or EV/EBITDA does not establish Riyaasat fair value. FY26 accounts are the common valuation earnings denominator, explicitly not an overlapping TTM or fabricated half-year cash-flow series. Current-year results, if available for individual peers, are not silently spliced into this common FY26 comparison.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue quality and organic growth\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | Core EBITDA | PAT | CFO | CFO/PAT | Gross debt | Inventory |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY23|20.93|1.81|1.32|-1.12|-0.85|1.02|5.27|\\n|FY24|22.88|5.35|4.08|-2.73|-0.67|4.74|14.97|\\n|FY25|24.80|6.42|4.87|1.49|0.31|8.87|21.40|\\n|FY26|33.37|10.19|5.19|-3.19|-0.61|41.36|41.05|\\n\\nAmounts are ₹crore. FY23–FY25 use the RHP restated series; FY26 uses the original annual report. This is a disclosed mixed-vintage bridge, not a fully restated four-year series. Original FY22–FY25 figures and subsequent comparatives remain separately available in inputs.\\n\\nFY24 revenue growth was 9.27%, FY25 8.43%, FY26 34.55%. The three-year endpoint CAGR is 16.82%, qualifying for REV7 without an upward discretionary adjustment. FY22 revenue ₹4.364602cr covers the incorporation stub, so a headline FY22–FY26 “four-year CAGR” would be misleading. The business is young, but a small absolute base is not itself grounds to punish growth mechanically.\\n\\nNo acquired operating business explains the expansion identified here. A property purchase is capital investment, not acquired revenue. Store openings are organic expansion, but not same-store growth. Price, units, customer counts, realized discount rates and audited SSSG are unavailable. Therefore nominal revenue growth is not called real volume growth or pricing power. Management’s existing-store growth expectations and contribution from new stores remain guidance, not independent proof. Strong FY26 revenue alongside inventory growth of roughly 92% raises a cash-demand question rather than confirming high-quality demand.\\n\\n## 7. Growth, reinvestment and incremental capital\\n\\nThe economic capital bridge is central. FY25 PPE was only ₹0.2927cr, but another ₹6.3775cr property advance was already in other noncurrent assets. Ignoring it would exaggerate subsequent returns. FY26 PPE reached ₹31.8852cr, inventory ₹41.0462cr and gross debt ₹41.3623cr. Gross PPE cash ₹32.7408cr is not the full net investing outflow: other noncurrent assets released ₹6.2336cr, giving net investing ₹26.5072cr. FY25’s₹4.8cr capital advance received in other current liabilities went tozero and its cash movement is not added a second time as capex. [RHP; AR26_BSE]\\n\\n`financial_calculations.json` shows financing capital (equity + debt − cash), a broad operating-capital reconstruction, their residual, normalized NOPAT at25% tax, average-capital returns, one-year changes and a three-elapsed-year FY23end→FY26end change. Broad operating capital includes identified property advances, supplier advances and deposits, excludes identified tax/prepaidIPO assets, and removes capital-advance financing from operating liabilities. Some earlier tax/advance splits remain inseparable, so residuals are visible and these are analytical proxies, not audited cohort ROIC.\\n\\nThe one-year incremental NOPAT/incremental operating-capital proxy is **6.13%**, and the three-year endpoint proxy **9.62%**. Both are timing-sensitive; FY26’s large investments were not productive for a full year, inventory is not independently validated, and original/restated vintages differ. A one-year-lagged FY26 NOPAT/FY25 capital diagnostic is also calculated, explicitly flagged as excluding much of the new investment and therefore potentially overstating returns. Commissioned-store cohort ROIC is ND because in-service dates and store-level NOPAT are unavailable. For a risky SME retailer, the analyst cost-of-capital range used here is 14%–18%, not an observed market quotation. Both the 6.13% one-year and 9.62% three-year proxies are below that range, although timing and reliability limitations prevent a definitive cohort-return conclusion. The full five-year incremental-return window is ND. Those limitations justify CAP NS rather than awarding a high return score from a small old capital denominator or treating negative CFO as merely “growth capex.”\\n\\n### Post-IPO capital plan, on a comparable basis\\n\\nOriginal four-store fitout and deposits totaled ₹13.5503cr, versus ₹12.47cr IPO allocation. The original rent schedule was not included in that capex total. September’s revised Mumbai and Ahmedabad headline table includes Ahmedabad annual rent ₹1.5732cr, so adding headline totals directly would double count rent already in modeled operating margins.\\n\\nAfter removing that annual rent, revised fitout is ₹17.2973cr and new deposits ₹1.9422cr, together ₹19.2395cr. Separately, the new Santacruz property cost ₹15.5cr. The fitout/deposit plan alone exceeds the unchanged IPO store allocation by ₹6.7695cr; including the additional property makes the incremental cash requirement much larger. This is not proof of current insolvency, but it makes current funding verification indispensable. [POSTAL; PROPERTY]\\n\\nOriginal Surat premises fell through after the owner cancelled on 15 July. Mumbai expansion replaces the original store location concept; Ahmedabad replaces Surat, while Hyderabad/Bengaluru remain. Shareholders approved the changes on 1 October, with results 3 October. The deadline moved to twelve months from approval, versus the original FY27 rollout. Sale certificate 22 September received 30 September supports a property event, not an independently confirmed cash-payment date; an earlier-payment sensitivity is retained.\\n\\n## 8. Management, governance and execution audit\\n\\nThe Galiya family’s7,885,428 promoter shares are about 73.39% of post-IPO issued shares. The postal ballot’s valid votes were 7,924,478, dominated by promoters, with 39,050 public votes and no votes against. Legal approval is relevant, but not independent commercial validation. Current promoter encumbrance and a comprehensive post-listing dilutive-instrument register are ND; absence of a new filing in this research is not proof of no pledge or no future dilution. [VOTE; RHP]\\n\\nExecution evidence is mixed: five stores sustained through FY25, a sixth Mumbai store and higher FY26 revenue are positive. Soon after listing, two store choices, comparable budget and completion timetable changed. The company’s statement in the RHP that it had no previous time/cost overrun describes that earlier record; it does not erase later scope escalation. The new owned-property plan may reduce rent or improve location quality, but those benefits need occupancy, throughput, cash-return and implementation evidence.\\n\\nOriginal FY23 accounts show ₹1.74cr advanced to Dhagaa, a firm in which all directors were partners, about 79% of original equity. The loan lacked a stipulated repayment/interest schedule. Subsequent related-party tables contain classification/summation inconsistencies. These are material governance questions, but no inference of siphoning is asserted without settlement and bank evidence. [AR2023, PDF22,38; RHP RPTnotes]\\n\\nOriginal FY26 directors remuneration ₹0.46cr, rent/commission ₹0.664cr, inconsistent KMP totals and CSR disclosure differences require reconciliation. The corporate-information page calls Sobhanaben independent while substantive disclosures identify her as the MD’s mother and WTD’s spouse. Treat the relationship as controlling evidence, not the erroneous label. Proper independents elsewhere in the governance section are not thereby assumed absent. [AR26_BSE]\\n\\nThe RHP records Arpan Chudgar’s6 June 2023 resignation for other assignments and successor appointment 3 July effective 1 July. Yet Arpan’s original FY23 opinion is dated 29 August 2023. The chronology needs original appointment/resignation filings. “No resignation during FY26” is not “no historical auditor resignation.” No causal accusation is inferred from the dates.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics and full audit sequence\\n\\nOriginal FY22–FY26 audits and all available CARO/IFC annexures were read. FY25 image-only audit page16 and the audit-trail paragraph were visually checked. The complete source-specific findings are retained in `audit_evidence.json`.\\n\\n- **FY22:** Arpan, unmodified; CARO/IFC stated exempt. The unusual going-concern emphasis says plans to improve but explicitly no uncertainty. It is not a material-uncertainty opinion.\\n- **FY23:** Arpan, unmodified main/IFC; inventory verification positive; Dhagaa on-demand/unfixed-term exposure. CARO’s no-lender-borrowing wording conflicts with the promoter-relative debt note. Same qualified-in-meaning, not modified-opinion, going-concern emphasis.\\n- **FY24:** Abhishek, unmodified main/IFC; explicit absence of audit-trail software in Other Matter. Inventory verification positive. CARO loan/no-loan language is internally inconsistent. No default/fraud/resignation reported.\\n- **FY25:** unmodified main/IFC; bank statements disagree with books. Audit-trail paragraph simultaneously says software not used and that the feature operated, unresolved contradictory wording. Inventory verification still positive.\\n- **FY26:** unmodified main/IFC and positive audit-trail paragraph, **but inventory verification impracticable**, bank/books mismatch continues, and inventory is outside Tally Prime. No reported fraud/default/wilful default/current-year auditor resignation; disputed income-tax ₹1.1812cr; no one-year liability-payment material uncertainty identified, which is not a future-funding guarantee.\\n\\n### Reconciliation rather than superficial red flags\\n\\n1. FY26 CFO is ₹−3.1932cr. The line labeled cash generated from operations is actually a working-capital subtotal. Adding₹10.4943cr operating profit and deducting ₹1.1073cr tax reconciles the final CFO within source rounding. This is a presentation defect, not evidence that final CFO fails arithmetic.\\n2. Original FY24 CFO₹−3.463378cr, FY25 comparative ₹−2.4215cr and RHP restated ₹−2.7276cr are different vintages. Original FY23 CFO₹−1.427597cr differs from restated ₹−1.1239cr. Original and comparative AR/AP balances also changed. All are preserved, not silently spliced.\\n3. Original FY24 investing subtotal incorrectly includes CFO. The cash roll works with actual PPE cash ₹0.328577cr, not the cumulative displayed investing number. This historic labeling problem is distinct from the FY26 subtotal issue.\\n4. CARO’s IPO count 2,702,400 is the net offer. Adding146,400 market-maker shares gives 2,848,800 fresh shares; ×₹106 gives ₹30.19728cr. No ₹3,020lakh proceeds is inferred from the smaller count.\\n5. The malformed note 39 intermediary wording contradicts the main management representation and is interrupted by an IPO table. Preserve it as an unresolved document issue, not a proven routing transaction.\\n6. FY26 inventory’s10%/20% haircuts would be ₹4.10462/₹8.20924cr before tax, around 22.5%/44.9% of audited equity. These are sensitivities, not estimates that those losses occurred.\\n7. Receivables are much smaller than inventory; reported net ₹0.8762cr is mainly less than six months old. This is contrary evidence to a generalized claim of all sales being uncollected. Supplier funding is substantial: payables ₹14.5673cr, including ₹2.0754cr MSME, mostly under one year. Neither assertion proves inventory value.\\n8. FY26 finance cost ₹3.0759cr includes interest ₹2.5611cr, bank charges ₹0.0414cr and other borrowing cost ₹0.4735cr. Core EBITDA adds back all finance cost and depreciation then removes other income. An interest-only addback is not a comparable EBITDA definition. A separate normalized PAT/current-share EPS bridge removes other income and applies 25% tax to core PBT. It does not invent an inventory adjustment; EARN stays NS.\\n\\nCumulative CFO/PAT uses sums, not averages. CFO is shown both before and after financing interest. Big PPE capex explains weak FCF, not negative CFO. Inventory absorbed ₹19.6435cr while suppliers provided ₹10.4128cr; advances absorbed another ₹1.7252cr. Opening current-tax provision ₹2.1289cr is a separate claim, not discharged merely by paying tax on future profits. The cash model pays it gross; excluded advance-tax credit ₹0.1961cr is not silently netted or valued.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\\n\\nAt the secondary ₹98.4 reference and 10,744,478 issued shares, conditional equity market cap is **₹105.73cr**. FY26 current-share EPS is **₹4.8342**, so P/E is **20.35×**. Using reported weighted-average EPS₹6.58 would overstate earnings per current share after the IPO. March debt/cash combined with October market cap produces a stale-balance EV diagnostic only; it is **not** current EV. Normalized FY26 PAT is ₹4.47225cr, current-share normalized EPS₹4.16237 and conditional normalized P/E **23.64×**. This removes ₹0.3071cr other income and applies 25% tax to ₹5.9630cr core PBT; no speculative inventory adjustment is included. Original FY25 PAT₹4.7824cr versus RHP restated ₹4.8664cr differs by ₹0.0840cr prior-year tax and is retained as a vintage bridge. The modeled term/CC components sum ₹41.3624cr versus reported debt ₹41.3623cr, a₹0.0001cr source rounding residual, not hidden financing. Gross IPO proceeds cannot be both added to entry cash and spent on projects. [AR26_BSE; SECURITY; PRICE]\\n\\n### Cash model and funding policy\\n\\nThe model starts 31 March 2026, not with an invented healthy October balance. It rolls dated sales, collections, supplier payments, inventory/receivables/payables, other operating capital, rent-inclusive EBITDA, tax, maintenance/growth capex, property, fitout/deposits, interest, debt principal and cash. It checks cash **before late collections** as well as closing cash. The reduced operating model opens with net assets of ₹17.8382cr, not a claim to restate audited equity of ₹18.2762cr: excluded advance-tax asset ₹0.1961cr, prepaid IPO asset ₹0.1826cr, DTA ₹0.0592cr and debt-component rounding ₹0.0001cr reconcile the difference. Prepaid IPO cost is recovered once in the fee bridge; other excluded tax assets receive no invented cash recovery. Initial restricted IPO balances are zero until 1 July. Net issue cash is allocated to store/WC/GCP pools and released only against matched uses. Prior-paid IPO fees are reimbursed once. The full fee budget is deducted at listing as a conservative early-payment/reservation assumption; the postal notice’s ₹0.9236cr unspent fee allocation is not treated as free project cash. Full reported WC/GCP use is enforced on1 September: unmatched restricted amounts are released against an equal explicit unreconciled-use outflow, leaving no spendable cash. The residual is tracked as memorandum deployed capital, not an assertion of an audited recoverable asset, and receives no separate terminal recovery. Its inclusion in capital employed prevents ignoring the cash already deployed. This conservative diagnostic may overlap with unidentified future operating balances; current accounts are required to resolve the allocation, not a hidden cash plug.\\n\\nOrdinary bear/base/bull allow **zero unverified new credit**. A later equity issue cannot repair an earlier pre-entry failure. Separate diagnostics assume explicitly uncommitted, capped ₹20cr, ₹25cr and ₹30cr pre-entry bridges,2% fee, scenario interest and full repayment 19 October 2028. These diagnostics do not establish that a lender offered such terms. The ₹20cr and ₹25cr cases still fail before entry at the property payment and cannot be repaired by entry equity. The separately labeled ₹30cr cases exercise later liquidity and terminal logic. The ordinary, ₹20cr and ₹25cr failed paths all remain intact.\\n\\nEntry rescue issues only whole shares, at 30% discount with 5% issue friction, bounded by 15,500,000 authorized less 10,744,478 existing = **4,755,522 shares**. Each feasible minimum is tested against the adjacent failing share. The authorization ceiling is not a guarantee of issuance approval or subscription. Lower entry prices also lower rescue proceeds, so a nominal cheap-price hurdle cannot be accepted without re-solving dilution and liquidity.\\n\\nThe table and root tests below use the separate ₹30cr diagnostic, not the actual funding case. Base forward inputs are ₹45cr entry revenue run-rate,18% annual growth,25% EBITDA after rent, inventory gradually falling to 75% of annual sales,30% payable/sales intensity,10 debtor days,13% interest and 25% normalized cash tax. Bear uses lower growth/margin and slower inventory conversion; bull uses 28% growth,30% margin and inventory 50% of sales. These are analytical assumptions, not management guidance. Principal uses disclosed first-year maturity and a finite assumed runoff; CC is repaid rather than automatically renewed. Exact contracts and current renewals remain ND.\\n\\n| Case | Ordinary | ₹30cr bridge + reference-price rescue | ₹30cr bridge + ₹200-price diagnostic | ₹30cr bridge + ₹300-price diagnostic |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n|bear|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-45.84% IRR; 3802838 rescue shares|\\n|base|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-26.01% IRR; 3187768 rescue shares|\\n|bull|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-3.46% IRR; 4657910 rescue shares|-9.06% IRR; 3105274 rescue shares|\\n\\n\\nThe ₹200 and ₹300 columns are deliberately **not** price targets. They test whether a higher-priced, same-authorized-share-cap fundraising can finance the model and what that does to the original investor’s return. A higher financing price can make the cash path feasible while making the shareholder return unattractive. A conditional funded path is not a favorable actual-funding conclusion.\\n\\n### Earned terminal value, not an arbitrary exit P/E\\n\\nThe horizon ends 19 October 2031, followed by a full twelve-month operating/cash continuation. Terminal ROIC is capped at the lower of an explicit ceiling and actual modeled NOPAT/invested capital, excluding cash; a separate one-month revenue cash reserve is also funded. Reinvestment is the greater of the physical capital-intensity requirement and NOPAT×g/ROIC. Reserve growth is separately retained and deducted from FCFF. Value = sustainable FCFF/(WACC−g), with positive spread and growth-return constraints. No terminal value is admitted unless the forward business meets intraperiod liquidity, reserve and no-new-financing self-funding tests.\\n\\nEnterprise value is bridged to common equity by debt/senior claims and only cash above the required operating reserve. Restricted funds are excluded. Residual debt service is not deducted twice through both a reserve and EV-to-equity bridge. No dividends are forecast, so retained cash is not also counted as distributed cash. Cash failures suppress ordinary IRR, even if an attractive suffix could be drawn after the failure.\\n\\n### Hurdles and sensitivities\\n\\nThe complete model retains **341 unique losslessly deduplicated paths** and all requested references, including failed ordinary paths, rescue bounds, adjacent failures, root trials, feasibility boundaries and forward schedules. It includes 12%/15%/18% joint price/dilution hurdles; reverse growth;5×/10× tests; growth-margin and WACC-terminal-growth grids; AR/inventory/payables; interest; collection timing; fitout overrun; six-month delay; earlier property payment; early CC repayment; inventory markdown; contingent-tax settlement and an explicitly optimistic productivity case. Exact inputs and root domains are visible.\\n\\n| Case | Hurdle | Joint entry-price result, domain ₹20–₹500 | Funding boundary |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|base|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|bull|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n\\n\\nAll six joint-price tests found no admissible return-hurdle bracket within ₹20–₹500. Funding first becomes feasible around ₹201.0986–201.1047 in the base and ₹195.8923–195.8954 in the bull diagnostic, but the funded shareholder returns do not meet 12%/15%/18%. These are fundraising feasibility boundaries, not buy prices. Reverse growth at the ₹98.4 reference found no admissible sampled hurdle bracket over 0%–80% annual growth, because bounded funding fails. The 5×/10× search over 0%–150% growth likewise establishes neither outcome; it is not a claim that every mathematically possible future is disproven.\\n\\n| Base sensitivity, separate ₹30cr bridge | Reference ₹98.4 | At ₹200: conditional IRR / rescue shares |\\n|---|---|---|\\n|margin_minus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|margin_plus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-15.09% / 4310194|\\n|inventory_50pct_sales|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-16.78% / 4173660|\\n|rate18pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|fitout_overrun25pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|optimistic_productivity|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-6.07% / 3109156|\\n|contingent_tax_paid|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n\\n\\nAt the reference price, even the displayed optimistic productivity case remains unfunded under the share cap. At₹200, lower inventory intensity improves the diagnostic but does not deliver the hurdle; a25% fitout overrun increases funding strain and 18% borrowing cost defeats bounded rescue.\\n\\nRoot searches are bounded sampled tests, not global proofs. An unfunded point is not assigned a negative IRR for interpolation. No root is interpolated across a funding discontinuity. Fixed-funding discount-back prices are shown only as diagnostics and retested through the joint funding framework. Execution-cost sensitivities use both entry and exit friction at 0%,1% and 3%. EV/NOPAT multiple-normalization tests are secondary sensitivities, not P/E tests of debt-free earnings; named peers do not justify a chosen terminal multiple. Terminal-value dependency is explicit because no interim shareholder cash distributions are assumed. A separate exact attribution identity decomposes price change into growth in normalized common-earnings proxy, share-count dilution and implied equity-multiple change. It is not a new valuation assumption; revenue scale is separately labeled.\\n\\n## 11. Strongest bear case and thesis-break variables\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not simply “small company.” It is that accounting profits precede cash by a very long period, fashion inventory is overstated or slow moving, suppliers/banks stop extending credit, and owned property plus expanding stores consume the IPO proceeds before new stores earn acceptable returns. A modest inventory impairment can absorb a large share of reported equity; a forced sale of merchandise or property may recover much less than book value. Debt-service timing then matters more than the exit multiple.\\n\\nThis case is falsifiable. Independently reconciled stock counts/aging/NRV, audited cash conversion, bank-book reconciliation and adequate committed facilities would weaken it. Mere revenue growth with still faster inventory growth would not. The model tests inventory book writeoff separately from cash replacement and lost-growth/margin stresses: a noncash markdown alone is not incorrectly modeled as an immediate cash payment, and accounting writeoff does not create sale proceeds.\\n\\nThesis-break indicators: inventory write-downs or unexplained inventory-age growth; continued bank mismatch; unfunded capex commitments; supplier/CC withdrawal; a material RPT increase without settlement; repeated unexplained share or fund-use discrepancies. None is represented as having occurred beyond the documented evidence.\\n\\n## 12. Five-times/ten-times and compounder test\\n\\nFive-times in five years requires 37.97% annual shareholder compounding; ten-times requires 58.49%. Revenue growth alone cannot produce those returns if additional working capital, fixed assets, financing costs and share dilution absorb the gains. The reverse paths solve for growth over a bounded domain while retaining all cash failures. No “5× potential” is awarded merely because a tiny initial base could mathematically expand.\\n\\nRiyaasat does not presently meet the v3 compounder test: real volume/pricing growth is not isolated, moat evidence is modest, incremental capital returns are unproven, cumulative cash conversion is weak, and critical accounting/funding/tradability gates remain open. Successful store expansion may eventually change that conclusion, but a turnaround in cash quality is a hypothesis, not a designation.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring and information needed\\n\\n| Priority | Evidence | What would change the assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Independently verified SKU stock, age, NRV and physical reconciliation | Determines inventory and earnings reliability; prerequisite for G2 release. |\\n| 1 | Bank quarterly/monthly reconciliations, sanction letters, utilization, covenants and exact maturity ladder | Determines usable headroom and G3; no reliance on gross sanctioned limits. |\\n| 1 |31 March→19 October cash roll, property payment schedule and all IPO-bank/utilization statements | Replaces pre-entry assumptions; identifies committed versus uncommitted funding. |\\n| 2 | Store-level revenue, area, opening dates, contribution after rent and cash invested | Tests organic growth, payback and incremental ROIC. |\\n| 2 | Corrected audit-trail, intermediary, KMP/CSR and RPT schedules; auditor succession filings | Resolves disclosure/control concerns proportionately. |\\n| 2 | Current share/option/warrant and encumbrance register | Confirms dilution and governance exposures. |\\n| 1 before execution | Verified BSE20/60-session complete calendar/value data, current lot/segment, spread/depth and restrictions | Allows L scoring and position-size execution test. |\\n\\nAt₹98.4 one 1,200-share lot costs ₹118,080 before fees; four lots ₹472,320 and five ₹590,400. The₹5lakh reference cannot be treated as an exactly divisible order. Entry/exit days at 2%/5%/10% participation are ND until both 20/60 means and medians are established, with genuine zero-trade days retained. Initial ten-day trade-for-trade arrangements do not establish October status. No order-book liquidity or price-band escape is assumed.\\n\\n## 14. Final classification and update discipline\\n\\n**Hold, unresolved. BQS NS, GQS NS (cap 5), IAS NS.** The score matrix cannot assign a numerical quality/attractiveness quadrant with missing canonical factors, and critical gates override an attractive-looking price. No compounder, multibagger or investable ranking designation is made. This is not an evidenced fraud/default “Avoid” classification, either.\\n\\nUpdate log,20 October cutoff: the RHP-only view is superseded by original FY22–FY26 audit reading and post-IPO project disclosures. Revenue evidence supports REV7; persistent cash weakness supports CASH2 and the GQS cap. CAP/EARN/BAL and all investment-attractiveness factors remain NS because material evidence is missing. Original-versus-restated cash figures, bank mismatch recurrence and inventory-system limitations are retained rather than overwritten. These are evidence/methodology clarifications, not a claim that the market price itself changed.\\n\\nNo batch winner or overall portfolio exit count is inferred. Rankings, if later requested, must use a common framework version, complete universe, explicit tie rules and gate eligibility. SME status remains distinct from the previously researched main-board universe.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, assumptions, recovery and final quality check\\n\\nSource IDs link to primary URLs, retrieval hashes and locations in `source_register.json`. Key source links: [RHP](https://aibi.org.in/Current_DOC/26270048.pdf), [issuer annual-report archive](https://www.riyaasatlifestyle.in/pages/annual-report), [FY26 exchange filing](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/95e91031-ad8c-484b-824d-eb4463efff51.pdf), [postal notice](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0780/2116/5367/files/Postal_Ballot_Notice.pdf?v=1788248084), [property disclosure](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/eb3a0341-f571-481c-8e13-f60b4824c89a.pdf), [voting result](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/5ad21c51-aa55-40a6-8664-c729f1b9beaa.pdf). Exchange documents retrieved via a public mirror retain the primary and mirror URLs plus exact byte hash. Peer sources and selected official market rows are retained separately.\\n\\nFactual extracts, management statements and analyst assumptions are distinct. All money inputs are crore unless expressly stated; source FY22–FY24 originals use thousands, RHP and FY25–FY26 use lakh, Vedant uses million. No overlapping TTM, invented quarterly cash flow or post-cutoff financial result is used. Missing is not zero. This is original analysis, not redistribution of source PDFs/OCR.\\n\\n### All sixteen final v3 checks\\n\\n1. Correct issuer/security/scope and dated price: checked; secondary execution limitation explicit.\\n2. Material primary records read; guidance and assumptions labeled: checked, full audit sequence included.\\n3. Audit/RPT/funding/dilution reviewed: checked; unresolved gaps remain gates, not fabricated clearance.\\n4. Periods/units/no overlapping TTM: checked; original/restated vintages separate.\\n5. Revenue versus earnings/acquisitions/base effects: checked; price/volume ND and startup stub excluded.\\n6. CFO/PAT ratio of sums; CFO versus capex: checked.\\n7. Incremental numerator/denominator/timing: visible; proxies qualified, five-year window ND.\\n8. Normalized tax/leases/claims/dilution: explicit; opening tax paid, no unsupported netting.\\n9. Bear/base/bull cash and financing: full traces retained, failures suppress returns.\\n10. Canonical factors and 100% weights: independently asserted.\\n11. NS/coverage/bounds/confidence: explicit, no renormalization.\\n12. Raw/final and override reconciliation: exact Decimal; stated cash cap and boundary tests only.\\n13. Complete-matrix classification/compounder criteria: NS/holds prevent designation.\\n14. Position-based liquidity: lot arithmetic completed; statistics/executable price still unresolved.\\n15. Strongest bear and thesis-break: explicit and falsifiable.\\n16. Common-version ranking/unfinished evidence: no investable ranking; gaps visibly unresolved.\\n\\nThe complete owned compact and full packages contain authored inputs and standard-library reconstruction code. Both regenerate the full canonical record, all failed paths, CSV ledger, score audit, calculations and report without original PDFs, network access or the other archive. A guarded clean rebuild deletes generated outputs **and validation/hash attestations**, then recreates them and checks against an external retained manifest. Source-safe packaging excludes downloaded PDFs/OCR/HTML, transfer helpers and private service identifiers. Technical reconstruction proves determinism and internal identities, not the economic truth of unverified issuer statements. Independent review and fresh Library-download tests must close before the package is treated as frozen.\\n\"","\"# Riyaasat Lifestyle Limited\\n## 1. Executive assessment\\n\\n**Hold, unresolved accounting, funding and tradability.** This is the ethnic-fashion retailer Riyaasat Lifestyle Limited, not a life insurer. The supplied label “Riyaasat Life” resolves to ISIN INE0KYI01012, BSE SME 544804, symbol RIYAASAT. No corporate name change was found: it was incorporated under its current name on 23 October 2021. The CIN prefix changed from unlisted U to listed L, not the business identity. [RHP, printed 199; SECURITY]\\n\\nThere is a genuine operating business and revenue growth, but the evidence does not establish a high-quality, self-financing compounder. Revenue rose from ₹20.9349cr in FY23 to ₹33.3746cr in FY26, a comparable three-year CAGR of **16.82%**. FY26 inventory was ₹41.0462cr, larger than annual revenue, with a physical-verification limitation and inventory maintained outside the main accounting software. Bank statements have not reconciled to books in two successive audits. Four-year disclosed cumulative CFO/PAT is **-0.359**, not an average of annual ratios. [RHP, printed 230–233; AR26_BSE, PDF60–61,69–71,96–97]\\n\\nThe listing raised ₹30.19728cr gross, not all cash still available in October. A subsequent ₹15.5cr property purchase and revised store commitments materially change the investment case. Current cash, available facilities and a reliable BSE trading history remain unverified. An apparently moderate **20.35×** current-share FY26 P/E therefore is not a margin-of-safety conclusion. No trade, position allocation or favorable investment ranking is recommended.\\n\\n**Decisive variable:** can independently verified inventory turn into cash without material markdowns while the expanded store plan is funded and generates returns above its cost of capital? The strongest competing interpretation is that inventory and owned property are investment ahead of profitable growth. That interpretation needs evidence, not an EBITDA extrapolation.\\n\\n## 2. Research header, gates and confidence\\n\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Market reference: **19 October 2026**. Later research retrieval dates do not extend the information cutoff.\\n- Scope: standalone company. FY26 directors report says no subsidiary, joint venture or associate company. Related-party “associate concerns” are not assumed to be equity-accounted investments.\\n- Accounting: Indian AS, Schedule III Division I, not IndAS. Store rent is an operating expense; do not graft lease-adjusted peer EBITDA multiples onto these accounts.\\n- Price: **₹98.4, rounded secondary 19 October close**, not a verified executable BSE price. Issued shares **10,744,478**, lot **1,200**, authorized shares **15,500,000**. The dated NSE interoperability master verifies security metadata, not an NSE listing or BSE turnover.\\n- Reference position: ₹5 lakh. Confidence: identity high; historical revenue medium-high; inventory/earnings and current funding low; execution low.\\n\\n| Gate | Assessment | Evidence and required resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Conditional | Family control, old related-party exposure and disclosure errors; no established fraud/default. Obtain corrected notes and succession/RPT schedules. |\\n| G2 Accounting | **Hold, unresolved** | FY26 inventory-verification limitation; repeated bank-versus-books mismatches; historical audit-trail weakness. Reconcile independently. |\\n| G3 Funding | **Hold, unresolved** | Very low audited cash, high debt, property and expanded store commitments; October facilities and cash unavailable. A simulation failure is not evidence of an actual default. |\\n| G4 Tradability | **Hold, unresolved** | 20/60-session BSE traded-value statistics, current spread/depth and surveillance status unavailable. Missing bars are not zero-trade observations. |\\n\\nThe audit reports’ unmodified main/IFC opinions are positive contrary evidence, not a reason to ignore their annexures. No actual G1 or G3 failure override is imposed merely because a conservative forward model fails.\\n\\n## 3. Canonical scorecard and scoring audit\\n\\n| Score | Result | Coverage | Known contribution | Uncapped bounds | Effective bounds |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|---|\\n|BQS|NS|80.00%|3.4600|3.66–5.46|3.66–5.46|\\n|GQS|NS|70.00%|4.0000|4.30–7.00|4.30–5.00|\\n|IAS|NS|0.00%|0.0000|1.00–10.00|1.00–10.00|\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Core reasoning | Contrary evidence / uncertainty |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Concentrated family control; recurring reporting/control weaknesses. | Unmodified opinions, no established fraud/default; shareholder approval for changed objects. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Local design/customization capability, but six-store scale, regional dependence and nonexclusive supply. | Premium mix and gross economics suggest a local franchise; no audited repeat-cohort advantage. |\\n| REV | 7 | 16.82% three-year nominal organic revenue CAGR; no acquired operating company identified. | Unit/price/SSSG decomposition ND; new store and short history matter. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Recurring wedding/festival use and broad men/women assortment. | Discretionary demand, fashion changes and geographic concentration. |\\n| CAP | NS | Investment base sharply expanded; property advances retained in invested capital. | New-capital cohort returns and inventory validity are insufficiently established. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Persistent weak cumulative conversion before capex; inventory consumes funds. | FY25 positive CFO, but capital-advance/tax classifications affect its quality. |\\n| BAL | NS | ₹41.36cr audited debt versus ₹0.128cr cash; major later commitments. | IPO provided capital; no default reported, but current usable liquidity ND. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Mumbai added; then IPO locations, comparable cost and deadline changed. | Approved changes may be economically sensible, but delivery/returns not yet demonstrated. |\\n| EARN | NS | Core EBITDA is reconstructed, not copied from inconsistent KPI definitions. | Inventory limitation prevents a reliable earnings-quality score. |\\n| R | NS | No verified funded base-case IRR. | Conditional simulations show requirements, not actual financing availability. |\\n| D | NS | Inventory haircut, supplier withdrawal and property illiquidity can impair equity. | Recoverable liquidation values and probabilities ND. |\\n| P | NS | Apparent P/E is measurable conditionally; current intrinsic value is not. | Accounting/funding uncertainty defeats a numeric margin-of-safety claim. |\\n| L | NS | Position/lot defined, required trading-value observations absent. | Listing and metadata do not establish executable volume. |\\n\\nThe actual v3 weights are used exactly. BQS: GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS: REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS: final unrounded BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. No missing factor is replaced with 5, and no weight is renormalized. Bound = known weighted contribution plus missing weight × [1,10]. Persistent CASH2 caps GQS at5, including its effective upper bound; it does **not** convert NS to a score. GOV3 and proven-G3-failure boundary tests are retained separately. Decimal arithmetic and half-up display rounding are used throughout. Each canonical factor’s supporting facts, contrary fact, source IDs and confidence are in `factor_evidence.json`.\\n\\nTwo uncertain scored factors are tested at ±1. GOV3/GOV5 boundary cases are retained; the severe-governance cap in the GOV3 case is hypothetical, not the current GOV4 judgment. Separately, MOAT3 lowers BQS raw bounds to 3.46–5.26, while MOAT5 raises them to 3.86–5.66. MOAT has no direct GQS weight, so GQS is unchanged. IAS remains NS in both cases; its informative nested bounds shift by −0.05 and +0.05 respectively through the 25% BQS weight. These sensitivities do not fill missing factors or establish investability.\\n\\n## 4. Business model and economic anatomy\\n\\nRiyaasat designs, manufactures through its production ecosystem, sources and retails ethnic apparel for men and women, including sherwanis, lehenga choli, Indo-western outfits, kurtas and Jodhpuris. The customer is primarily an occasionwear buyer, not a contracted industrial customer. Customization, local taste, assortment and the in-store experience matter. Merchandise ownership, physical stores and fashion obsolescence make this a cash-intensive retail model, not an asset-light brand royalty stream. [RHP, business section 151–198]\\n\\nManufacturing represented about 35.06% of FY25 revenue and trading 64.94%; the January 2026 mix moved to roughly 25.58% manufacturing. This is not pure manufacturing growth. FY25’s five leading categories represented 74.09% of sales: sherwani 19.84%, lehenga choli 19.19%, Indo-western 16.02%, kurta 12.13% and Jodhpuri6.91%. A changing mix can alter margins without demonstrating price increases. B2B selling through PSL Retail/Pernia’s Pop-Up is disclosed, but no audited channel-profit or repeat-customer cohort is available. [RHP]\\n\\nThe disclosed six stores total about 25,900sqft: four Ahmedabad locations, Vadodara and owned Santacruz Mumbai. Five stores were present in FY23–FY25; Mumbai made six in FY26. RHP milestone language and store-opening tables are not perfectly aligned on initial timing, so exact per-store cohorts are not manufactured. Gujarat accounted for 96.59% of FY25 revenue and 67.13% of the January 2026 period; Maharashtra and other channels explain much of the mix shift. No export-led scaling thesis is established.\\n\\nDirect materials/traded merchandise plus separately disclosed manufacturing expenses are combined into a broad COGS proxy. The naïve FY26 sales less material/purchases/inventory-change calculation overstates gross margin because job work, labour and consumables sit in other expenses. The broad FY26 COGS proxy is ₹10.2653cr and gross margin about 69.24%, still subject to payroll-allocation and inventory-valuation limits. Rent of ₹3.1424cr is already expensed.\\n\\n## 5. Competitive position and named economic peers\\n\\nThe local franchise is credible, but durable pricing power is not demonstrated by one year’s high accounting margin. There are no exclusive supplier arrangements, and expansion outside familiar western markets requires different demand knowledge. Disclosed supplier-concentration table headings and percentage denominators are inconsistent: the stated percentages cannot simply be treated as percentages of revenue. No precise supplier-share claim is inferred from that mislabeled denominator. [RHP, printed 24–25]\\n\\n| Peer | FY26 revenue | FY26 PAT | Raw CFO/PAT | 19 Oct price | P/E |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|Vedant Fashions Limited|1435.50|375.50|1.28|509.85|32.99|\\n|Go Fashion (India) Limited|838.01|59.18|2.78|265.40|23.59|\\n|Sai Silks (Kalamandir) Limited|1653.67|140.92|2.29|72.54|7.89|\\n\\nAll peer prices above are from the official **19 October 2026 NSE bhavcopy**, and FY26 operating facts come from primary reports, not an aggregator comparison. [PEERMARKET; VEDANT26; GO26; SAI26]\\n\\n- **Vedant Fashions:** closest weddingwear/men’s occasionwear brand comparison, but 669 EBOs and franchise/wholesale economics differ sharply. FY26 reported revenue grew 3.5%, retail customer sales 6.1%, SSSG2.7%. Raw CFO/PAT is about 128%, not the presentation’s adjusted 98% cash-conversion metric. Lease principal plus interest was ₹192.4cr and capex ₹12.3cr. Large financial assets, brand reach and different capital ownership mean its multiple is not transferable.\\n- **Go Fashion:** useful store/inventory execution comparator, not a weddingwear clone. Revenue declined, PAT fell and cash-flow optics are affected by IndAS leases. CFO₹164.7048cr less lease cash ₹139.3933cr and capex ₹39.1573cr leaves negative cash. The primary FY26 report has 52,595,984 post-buyback shares; the dated exchange security master still carries 54,008,984. Both denominator valuations are retained rather than silently adopting a stale master. Weighted EPS is not used for current market cap.\\n- **Sai Silks:** ethnic-fashion retail with 81 stores and 3% SSSG, but different regional, women’s and store-size mix. ₹322.52cr CFO benefited from a₹137.14cr payable increase. Subtract₹50.64cr capex and ₹43.90cr lease principal; finance cash ₹30.98cr is separately disclosed. Do not compare this principal-only lease deduction directly with peers’ all-in lease cash without that adjustment.\\n\\nThese primary peers show that same-store growth can be far below new-store growth, inventory ties up cash, and rent accounting matters. A simple average P/E or EV/EBITDA does not establish Riyaasat fair value. FY26 accounts are the common valuation earnings denominator, explicitly not an overlapping TTM or fabricated half-year cash-flow series. Current-year results, if available for individual peers, are not silently spliced into this common FY26 comparison.\\n\\n## 6. Revenue quality and organic growth\\n\\n| Year | Revenue | Core EBITDA | PAT | CFO | CFO/PAT | Gross debt | Inventory |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY23|20.93|1.81|1.32|-1.12|-0.85|1.02|5.27|\\n|FY24|22.88|5.35|4.08|-2.73|-0.67|4.74|14.97|\\n|FY25|24.80|6.42|4.87|1.49|0.31|8.87|21.40|\\n|FY26|33.37|10.19|5.19|-3.19|-0.61|41.36|41.05|\\n\\nAmounts are ₹crore. FY23–FY25 use the RHP restated series; FY26 uses the original annual report. This is a disclosed mixed-vintage bridge, not a fully restated four-year series. Original FY22–FY25 figures and subsequent comparatives remain separately available in inputs.\\n\\nFY24 revenue growth was 9.27%, FY25 8.43%, FY26 34.55%. The three-year endpoint CAGR is 16.82%, qualifying for REV7 without an upward discretionary adjustment. FY22 revenue ₹4.364602cr covers the incorporation stub, so a headline FY22–FY26 “four-year CAGR” would be misleading. The business is young, but a small absolute base is not itself grounds to punish growth mechanically.\\n\\nNo acquired operating business explains the expansion identified here. A property purchase is capital investment, not acquired revenue. Store openings are organic expansion, but not same-store growth. Price, units, customer counts, realized discount rates and audited SSSG are unavailable. Therefore nominal revenue growth is not called real volume growth or pricing power. Management’s existing-store growth expectations and contribution from new stores remain guidance, not independent proof. Strong FY26 revenue alongside inventory growth of roughly 92% raises a cash-demand question rather than confirming high-quality demand.\\n\\n## 7. Growth, reinvestment and incremental capital\\n\\nThe economic capital bridge is central. FY25 PPE was only ₹0.2927cr, but another ₹6.3775cr property advance was already in other noncurrent assets. Ignoring it would exaggerate subsequent returns. FY26 PPE reached ₹31.8852cr, inventory ₹41.0462cr and gross debt ₹41.3623cr. Gross PPE cash ₹32.7408cr is not the full net investing outflow: other noncurrent assets released ₹6.2336cr, giving net investing ₹26.5072cr. FY25’s₹4.8cr capital advance received in other current liabilities went tozero and its cash movement is not added a second time as capex. [RHP; AR26_BSE]\\n\\n`financial_calculations.json` shows financing capital (equity + debt − cash), a broad operating-capital reconstruction, their residual, normalized NOPAT at25% tax, average-capital returns, one-year changes and a three-elapsed-year FY23end→FY26end change. Broad operating capital includes identified property advances, supplier advances and deposits, excludes identified tax/prepaidIPO assets, and removes capital-advance financing from operating liabilities. Some earlier tax/advance splits remain inseparable, so residuals are visible and these are analytical proxies, not audited cohort ROIC.\\n\\nThe one-year incremental NOPAT/incremental operating-capital proxy is **6.13%**, and the three-year endpoint proxy **9.62%**. Both are timing-sensitive; FY26’s large investments were not productive for a full year, inventory is not independently validated, and original/restated vintages differ. A one-year-lagged FY26 NOPAT/FY25 capital diagnostic is also calculated, explicitly flagged as excluding much of the new investment and therefore potentially overstating returns. Commissioned-store cohort ROIC is ND because in-service dates and store-level NOPAT are unavailable. For a risky SME retailer, the analyst cost-of-capital range used here is 14%–18%, not an observed market quotation. Both the 6.13% one-year and 9.62% three-year proxies are below that range, although timing and reliability limitations prevent a definitive cohort-return conclusion. The full five-year incremental-return window is ND. Those limitations justify CAP NS rather than awarding a high return score from a small old capital denominator or treating negative CFO as merely “growth capex.”\\n\\n### Post-IPO capital plan, on a comparable basis\\n\\nOriginal four-store fitout and deposits totaled ₹13.5503cr, versus ₹12.47cr IPO allocation. The original rent schedule was not included in that capex total. September’s revised Mumbai and Ahmedabad headline table includes Ahmedabad annual rent ₹1.5732cr, so adding headline totals directly would double count rent already in modeled operating margins.\\n\\nAfter removing that annual rent, revised fitout is ₹17.2973cr and new deposits ₹1.9422cr, together ₹19.2395cr. Separately, the new Santacruz property cost ₹15.5cr. The fitout/deposit plan alone exceeds the unchanged IPO store allocation by ₹6.7695cr; including the additional property makes the incremental cash requirement much larger. This is not proof of current insolvency, but it makes current funding verification indispensable. [POSTAL; PROPERTY]\\n\\nOriginal Surat premises fell through after the owner cancelled on 15 July. Mumbai expansion replaces the original store location concept; Ahmedabad replaces Surat, while Hyderabad/Bengaluru remain. Shareholders approved the changes on 1 October, with results 3 October. The deadline moved to twelve months from approval, versus the original FY27 rollout. Sale certificate 22 September received 30 September supports a property event, not an independently confirmed cash-payment date; an earlier-payment sensitivity is retained.\\n\\n## 8. Management, governance and execution audit\\n\\nThe Galiya family’s7,885,428 promoter shares are about 73.39% of post-IPO issued shares. The postal ballot’s valid votes were 7,924,478, dominated by promoters, with 39,050 public votes and no votes against. Legal approval is relevant, but not independent commercial validation. Current promoter encumbrance and a comprehensive post-listing dilutive-instrument register are ND; absence of a new filing in this research is not proof of no pledge or no future dilution. [VOTE; RHP]\\n\\nExecution evidence is mixed: five stores sustained through FY25, a sixth Mumbai store and higher FY26 revenue are positive. Soon after listing, two store choices, comparable budget and completion timetable changed. The company’s statement in the RHP that it had no previous time/cost overrun describes that earlier record; it does not erase later scope escalation. The new owned-property plan may reduce rent or improve location quality, but those benefits need occupancy, throughput, cash-return and implementation evidence.\\n\\nOriginal FY23 accounts show ₹1.74cr advanced to Dhagaa, a firm in which all directors were partners, about 79% of original equity. The loan lacked a stipulated repayment/interest schedule. Subsequent related-party tables contain classification/summation inconsistencies. These are material governance questions, but no inference of siphoning is asserted without settlement and bank evidence. [AR2023, PDF22,38; RHP RPTnotes]\\n\\nOriginal FY26 directors remuneration ₹0.46cr, rent/commission ₹0.664cr, inconsistent KMP totals and CSR disclosure differences require reconciliation. The corporate-information page calls Sobhanaben independent while substantive disclosures identify her as the MD’s mother and WTD’s spouse. Treat the relationship as controlling evidence, not the erroneous label. Proper independents elsewhere in the governance section are not thereby assumed absent. [AR26_BSE]\\n\\nThe RHP records Arpan Chudgar’s6 June 2023 resignation for other assignments and successor appointment 3 July effective 1 July. Yet Arpan’s original FY23 opinion is dated 29 August 2023. The chronology needs original appointment/resignation filings. “No resignation during FY26” is not “no historical auditor resignation.” No causal accusation is inferred from the dates.\\n\\n## 9. Financial forensics and full audit sequence\\n\\nOriginal FY22–FY26 audits and all available CARO/IFC annexures were read. FY25 image-only audit page16 and the audit-trail paragraph were visually checked. The complete source-specific findings are retained in `audit_evidence.json`.\\n\\n- **FY22:** Arpan, unmodified; CARO/IFC stated exempt. The unusual going-concern emphasis says plans to improve but explicitly no uncertainty. It is not a material-uncertainty opinion.\\n- **FY23:** Arpan, unmodified main/IFC; inventory verification positive; Dhagaa on-demand/unfixed-term exposure. CARO’s no-lender-borrowing wording conflicts with the promoter-relative debt note. Same qualified-in-meaning, not modified-opinion, going-concern emphasis.\\n- **FY24:** Abhishek, unmodified main/IFC; explicit absence of audit-trail software in Other Matter. Inventory verification positive. CARO loan/no-loan language is internally inconsistent. No default/fraud/resignation reported.\\n- **FY25:** unmodified main/IFC; bank statements disagree with books. Audit-trail paragraph simultaneously says software not used and that the feature operated, unresolved contradictory wording. Inventory verification still positive.\\n- **FY26:** unmodified main/IFC and positive audit-trail paragraph, **but inventory verification impracticable**, bank/books mismatch continues, and inventory is outside Tally Prime. No reported fraud/default/wilful default/current-year auditor resignation; disputed income-tax ₹1.1812cr; no one-year liability-payment material uncertainty identified, which is not a future-funding guarantee.\\n\\n### Reconciliation rather than superficial red flags\\n\\n1. FY26 CFO is ₹−3.1932cr. The line labeled cash generated from operations is actually a working-capital subtotal. Adding₹10.4943cr operating profit and deducting ₹1.1073cr tax reconciles the final CFO within source rounding. This is a presentation defect, not evidence that final CFO fails arithmetic.\\n2. Original FY24 CFO₹−3.463378cr, FY25 comparative ₹−2.4215cr and RHP restated ₹−2.7276cr are different vintages. Original FY23 CFO₹−1.427597cr differs from restated ₹−1.1239cr. Original and comparative AR/AP balances also changed. All are preserved, not silently spliced.\\n3. Original FY24 investing subtotal incorrectly includes CFO. The cash roll works with actual PPE cash ₹0.328577cr, not the cumulative displayed investing number. This historic labeling problem is distinct from the FY26 subtotal issue.\\n4. CARO’s IPO count 2,702,400 is the net offer. Adding146,400 market-maker shares gives 2,848,800 fresh shares; ×₹106 gives ₹30.19728cr. No ₹3,020lakh proceeds is inferred from the smaller count.\\n5. The malformed note 39 intermediary wording contradicts the main management representation and is interrupted by an IPO table. Preserve it as an unresolved document issue, not a proven routing transaction.\\n6. FY26 inventory’s10%/20% haircuts would be ₹4.10462/₹8.20924cr before tax, around 22.5%/44.9% of audited equity. These are sensitivities, not estimates that those losses occurred.\\n7. Receivables are much smaller than inventory; reported net ₹0.8762cr is mainly less than six months old. This is contrary evidence to a generalized claim of all sales being uncollected. Supplier funding is substantial: payables ₹14.5673cr, including ₹2.0754cr MSME, mostly under one year. Neither assertion proves inventory value.\\n8. FY26 finance cost ₹3.0759cr includes interest ₹2.5611cr, bank charges ₹0.0414cr and other borrowing cost ₹0.4735cr. Core EBITDA adds back all finance cost and depreciation then removes other income. An interest-only addback is not a comparable EBITDA definition. A separate normalized PAT/current-share EPS bridge removes other income and applies 25% tax to core PBT. It does not invent an inventory adjustment; EARN stays NS.\\n\\nCumulative CFO/PAT uses sums, not averages. CFO is shown both before and after financing interest. Big PPE capex explains weak FCF, not negative CFO. Inventory absorbed ₹19.6435cr while suppliers provided ₹10.4128cr; advances absorbed another ₹1.7252cr. Opening current-tax provision ₹2.1289cr is a separate claim, not discharged merely by paying tax on future profits. The cash model pays it gross; excluded advance-tax credit ₹0.1961cr is not silently netted or valued.\\n\\n## 10. Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\\n\\nAt the secondary ₹98.4 reference and 10,744,478 issued shares, conditional equity market cap is **₹105.73cr**. FY26 current-share EPS is **₹4.8342**, so P/E is **20.35×**. Using reported weighted-average EPS₹6.58 would overstate earnings per current share after the IPO. March debt/cash combined with October market cap produces a stale-balance EV diagnostic only; it is **not** current EV. Normalized FY26 PAT is ₹4.47225cr, current-share normalized EPS₹4.16237 and conditional normalized P/E **23.64×**. This removes ₹0.3071cr other income and applies 25% tax to ₹5.9630cr core PBT; no speculative inventory adjustment is included. Original FY25 PAT₹4.7824cr versus RHP restated ₹4.8664cr differs by ₹0.0840cr prior-year tax and is retained as a vintage bridge. The modeled term/CC components sum ₹41.3624cr versus reported debt ₹41.3623cr, a₹0.0001cr source rounding residual, not hidden financing. Gross IPO proceeds cannot be both added to entry cash and spent on projects. [AR26_BSE; SECURITY; PRICE]\\n\\n### Cash model and funding policy\\n\\nThe model starts 31 March 2026, not with an invented healthy October balance. It rolls dated sales, collections, supplier payments, inventory/receivables/payables, other operating capital, rent-inclusive EBITDA, tax, maintenance/growth capex, property, fitout/deposits, interest, debt principal and cash. It checks cash **before late collections** as well as closing cash. The reduced operating model opens with net assets of ₹17.8382cr, not a claim to restate audited equity of ₹18.2762cr: excluded advance-tax asset ₹0.1961cr, prepaid IPO asset ₹0.1826cr, DTA ₹0.0592cr and debt-component rounding ₹0.0001cr reconcile the difference. Prepaid IPO cost is recovered once in the fee bridge; other excluded tax assets receive no invented cash recovery. Initial restricted IPO balances are zero until 1 July. Net issue cash is allocated to store/WC/GCP pools and released only against matched uses. Prior-paid IPO fees are reimbursed once. The full fee budget is deducted at listing as a conservative early-payment/reservation assumption; the postal notice’s ₹0.9236cr unspent fee allocation is not treated as free project cash. Full reported WC/GCP use is enforced on1 September: unmatched restricted amounts are released against an equal explicit unreconciled-use outflow, leaving no spendable cash. The residual is tracked as memorandum deployed capital, not an assertion of an audited recoverable asset, and receives no separate terminal recovery. Its inclusion in capital employed prevents ignoring the cash already deployed. This conservative diagnostic may overlap with unidentified future operating balances; current accounts are required to resolve the allocation, not a hidden cash plug.\\n\\nOrdinary bear/base/bull allow **zero unverified new credit**. A later equity issue cannot repair an earlier pre-entry failure. Separate diagnostics assume explicitly uncommitted, capped ₹20cr, ₹25cr and ₹30cr pre-entry bridges,2% fee, scenario interest and full repayment 19 October 2028. These diagnostics do not establish that a lender offered such terms. The ₹20cr and ₹25cr cases still fail before entry at the property payment and cannot be repaired by entry equity. The separately labeled ₹30cr cases exercise later liquidity and terminal logic. The ordinary, ₹20cr and ₹25cr failed paths all remain intact.\\n\\nEntry rescue issues only whole shares, at 30% discount with 5% issue friction, bounded by 15,500,000 authorized less 10,744,478 existing = **4,755,522 shares**. Each feasible minimum is tested against the adjacent failing share. The authorization ceiling is not a guarantee of issuance approval or subscription. Lower entry prices also lower rescue proceeds, so a nominal cheap-price hurdle cannot be accepted without re-solving dilution and liquidity.\\n\\nThe table and root tests below use the separate ₹30cr diagnostic, not the actual funding case. Base forward inputs are ₹45cr entry revenue run-rate,18% annual growth,25% EBITDA after rent, inventory gradually falling to 75% of annual sales,30% payable/sales intensity,10 debtor days,13% interest and 25% normalized cash tax. Bear uses lower growth/margin and slower inventory conversion; bull uses 28% growth,30% margin and inventory 50% of sales. These are analytical assumptions, not management guidance. Principal uses disclosed first-year maturity and a finite assumed runoff; CC is repaid rather than automatically renewed. Exact contracts and current renewals remain ND.\\n\\n| Case | Ordinary | ₹30cr bridge + reference-price rescue | ₹30cr bridge + ₹200-price diagnostic | ₹30cr bridge + ₹300-price diagnostic |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n|bear|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-45.84% IRR; 3802838 rescue shares|\\n|base|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-26.01% IRR; 3187768 rescue shares|\\n|bull|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-3.46% IRR; 4657910 rescue shares|-9.06% IRR; 3105274 rescue shares|\\n\\n\\nThe ₹200 and ₹300 columns are deliberately **not** price targets. They test whether a higher-priced, same-authorized-share-cap fundraising can finance the model and what that does to the original investor’s return. A higher financing price can make the cash path feasible while making the shareholder return unattractive. A conditional funded path is not a favorable actual-funding conclusion.\\n\\n### Earned terminal value, not an arbitrary exit P/E\\n\\nThe horizon ends 19 October 2031, followed by a full twelve-month operating/cash continuation. Terminal ROIC is capped at the lower of an explicit ceiling and actual modeled NOPAT/invested capital, excluding cash; a separate one-month revenue cash reserve is also funded. Reinvestment is the greater of the physical capital-intensity requirement and NOPAT×g/ROIC. Reserve growth is separately retained and deducted from FCFF. Value = sustainable FCFF/(WACC−g), with positive spread and growth-return constraints. No terminal value is admitted unless the forward business meets intraperiod liquidity, reserve and no-new-financing self-funding tests.\\n\\nEnterprise value is bridged to common equity by debt/senior claims and only cash above the required operating reserve. Restricted funds are excluded. Residual debt service is not deducted twice through both a reserve and EV-to-equity bridge. No dividends are forecast, so retained cash is not also counted as distributed cash. Cash failures suppress ordinary IRR, even if an attractive suffix could be drawn after the failure.\\n\\n### Hurdles and sensitivities\\n\\nThe complete model retains **341 unique losslessly deduplicated paths** and all requested references, including failed ordinary paths, rescue bounds, adjacent failures, root trials, feasibility boundaries and forward schedules. It includes 12%/15%/18% joint price/dilution hurdles; reverse growth;5×/10× tests; growth-margin and WACC-terminal-growth grids; AR/inventory/payables; interest; collection timing; fitout overrun; six-month delay; earlier property payment; early CC repayment; inventory markdown; contingent-tax settlement and an explicitly optimistic productivity case. Exact inputs and root domains are visible.\\n\\n| Case | Hurdle | Joint entry-price result, domain ₹20–₹500 | Funding boundary |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|base|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|bull|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n\\n\\nAll six joint-price tests found no admissible return-hurdle bracket within ₹20–₹500. Funding first becomes feasible around ₹201.0986–201.1047 in the base and ₹195.8923–195.8954 in the bull diagnostic, but the funded shareholder returns do not meet 12%/15%/18%. These are fundraising feasibility boundaries, not buy prices. Reverse growth at the ₹98.4 reference found no admissible sampled hurdle bracket over 0%–80% annual growth, because bounded funding fails. The 5×/10× search over 0%–150% growth likewise establishes neither outcome; it is not a claim that every mathematically possible future is disproven.\\n\\n| Base sensitivity, separate ₹30cr bridge | Reference ₹98.4 | At ₹200: conditional IRR / rescue shares |\\n|---|---|---|\\n|margin_minus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|margin_plus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-15.09% / 4310194|\\n|inventory_50pct_sales|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-16.78% / 4173660|\\n|rate18pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|fitout_overrun25pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|optimistic_productivity|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-6.07% / 3109156|\\n|contingent_tax_paid|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n\\n\\nAt the reference price, even the displayed optimistic productivity case remains unfunded under the share cap. At₹200, lower inventory intensity improves the diagnostic but does not deliver the hurdle; a25% fitout overrun increases funding strain and 18% borrowing cost defeats bounded rescue.\\n\\nRoot searches are bounded sampled tests, not global proofs. An unfunded point is not assigned a negative IRR for interpolation. No root is interpolated across a funding discontinuity. Fixed-funding discount-back prices are shown only as diagnostics and retested through the joint funding framework. Execution-cost sensitivities use both entry and exit friction at 0%,1% and 3%. EV/NOPAT multiple-normalization tests are secondary sensitivities, not P/E tests of debt-free earnings; named peers do not justify a chosen terminal multiple. Terminal-value dependency is explicit because no interim shareholder cash distributions are assumed. A separate exact attribution identity decomposes price change into growth in normalized common-earnings proxy, share-count dilution and implied equity-multiple change. It is not a new valuation assumption; revenue scale is separately labeled.\\n\\n## 11. Strongest bear case and thesis-break variables\\n\\nThe strongest bear case is not simply “small company.” It is that accounting profits precede cash by a very long period, fashion inventory is overstated or slow moving, suppliers/banks stop extending credit, and owned property plus expanding stores consume the IPO proceeds before new stores earn acceptable returns. A modest inventory impairment can absorb a large share of reported equity; a forced sale of merchandise or property may recover much less than book value. Debt-service timing then matters more than the exit multiple.\\n\\nThis case is falsifiable. Independently reconciled stock counts/aging/NRV, audited cash conversion, bank-book reconciliation and adequate committed facilities would weaken it. Mere revenue growth with still faster inventory growth would not. The model tests inventory book writeoff separately from cash replacement and lost-growth/margin stresses: a noncash markdown alone is not incorrectly modeled as an immediate cash payment, and accounting writeoff does not create sale proceeds.\\n\\nThesis-break indicators: inventory write-downs or unexplained inventory-age growth; continued bank mismatch; unfunded capex commitments; supplier/CC withdrawal; a material RPT increase without settlement; repeated unexplained share or fund-use discrepancies. None is represented as having occurred beyond the documented evidence.\\n\\n## 12. Five-times/ten-times and compounder test\\n\\nFive-times in five years requires 37.97% annual shareholder compounding; ten-times requires 58.49%. Revenue growth alone cannot produce those returns if additional working capital, fixed assets, financing costs and share dilution absorb the gains. The reverse paths solve for growth over a bounded domain while retaining all cash failures. No “5× potential” is awarded merely because a tiny initial base could mathematically expand.\\n\\nRiyaasat does not presently meet the v3 compounder test: real volume/pricing growth is not isolated, moat evidence is modest, incremental capital returns are unproven, cumulative cash conversion is weak, and critical accounting/funding/tradability gates remain open. Successful store expansion may eventually change that conclusion, but a turnaround in cash quality is a hypothesis, not a designation.\\n\\n## 13. Monitoring and information needed\\n\\n| Priority | Evidence | What would change the assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Independently verified SKU stock, age, NRV and physical reconciliation | Determines inventory and earnings reliability; prerequisite for G2 release. |\\n| 1 | Bank quarterly/monthly reconciliations, sanction letters, utilization, covenants and exact maturity ladder | Determines usable headroom and G3; no reliance on gross sanctioned limits. |\\n| 1 |31 March→19 October cash roll, property payment schedule and all IPO-bank/utilization statements | Replaces pre-entry assumptions; identifies committed versus uncommitted funding. |\\n| 2 | Store-level revenue, area, opening dates, contribution after rent and cash invested | Tests organic growth, payback and incremental ROIC. |\\n| 2 | Corrected audit-trail, intermediary, KMP/CSR and RPT schedules; auditor succession filings | Resolves disclosure/control concerns proportionately. |\\n| 2 | Current share/option/warrant and encumbrance register | Confirms dilution and governance exposures. |\\n| 1 before execution | Verified BSE20/60-session complete calendar/value data, current lot/segment, spread/depth and restrictions | Allows L scoring and position-size execution test. |\\n\\nAt₹98.4 one 1,200-share lot costs ₹118,080 before fees; four lots ₹472,320 and five ₹590,400. The₹5lakh reference cannot be treated as an exactly divisible order. Entry/exit days at 2%/5%/10% participation are ND until both 20/60 means and medians are established, with genuine zero-trade days retained. Initial ten-day trade-for-trade arrangements do not establish October status. No order-book liquidity or price-band escape is assumed.\\n\\n## 14. Final classification and update discipline\\n\\n**Hold, unresolved. BQS NS, GQS NS (cap 5), IAS NS.** The score matrix cannot assign a numerical quality/attractiveness quadrant with missing canonical factors, and critical gates override an attractive-looking price. No compounder, multibagger or investable ranking designation is made. This is not an evidenced fraud/default “Avoid” classification, either.\\n\\nUpdate log,20 October cutoff: the RHP-only view is superseded by original FY22–FY26 audit reading and post-IPO project disclosures. Revenue evidence supports REV7; persistent cash weakness supports CASH2 and the GQS cap. CAP/EARN/BAL and all investment-attractiveness factors remain NS because material evidence is missing. Original-versus-restated cash figures, bank mismatch recurrence and inventory-system limitations are retained rather than overwritten. These are evidence/methodology clarifications, not a claim that the market price itself changed.\\n\\nNo batch winner or overall portfolio exit count is inferred. Rankings, if later requested, must use a common framework version, complete universe, explicit tie rules and gate eligibility. SME status remains distinct from the previously researched main-board universe.\\n\\n## 15. Sources, assumptions, recovery and final quality check\\n\\nSource IDs link to primary URLs, retrieval hashes and locations in `source_register.json`. Key source links: [RHP](https://aibi.org.in/Current_DOC/26270048.pdf), [issuer annual-report archive](https://www.riyaasatlifestyle.in/pages/annual-report), [FY26 exchange filing](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/95e91031-ad8c-484b-824d-eb4463efff51.pdf), [postal notice](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0780/2116/5367/files/Postal_Ballot_Notice.pdf?v=1788248084), [property disclosure](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/eb3a0341-f571-481c-8e13-f60b4824c89a.pdf), [voting result](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/5ad21c51-aa55-40a6-8664-c729f1b9beaa.pdf). Exchange documents retrieved via a public mirror retain the primary and mirror URLs plus exact byte hash. Peer sources and selected official market rows are retained separately.\\n\\nFactual extracts, management statements and analyst assumptions are distinct. All money inputs are crore unless expressly stated; source FY22–FY24 originals use thousands, RHP and FY25–FY26 use lakh, Vedant uses million. No overlapping TTM, invented quarterly cash flow or post-cutoff financial result is used. Missing is not zero. This is original analysis, not redistribution of source PDFs/OCR.\\n\\n### All sixteen final v3 checks\\n\\n1. Correct issuer/security/scope and dated price: checked; secondary execution limitation explicit.\\n2. Material primary records read; guidance and assumptions labeled: checked, full audit sequence included.\\n3. Audit/RPT/funding/dilution reviewed: checked; unresolved gaps remain gates, not fabricated clearance.\\n4. Periods/units/no overlapping TTM: checked; original/restated vintages separate.\\n5. Revenue versus earnings/acquisitions/base effects: checked; price/volume ND and startup stub excluded.\\n6. CFO/PAT ratio of sums; CFO versus capex: checked.\\n7. Incremental numerator/denominator/timing: visible; proxies qualified, five-year window ND.\\n8. Normalized tax/leases/claims/dilution: explicit; opening tax paid, no unsupported netting.\\n9. Bear/base/bull cash and financing: full traces retained, failures suppress returns.\\n10. Canonical factors and 100% weights: independently asserted.\\n11. NS/coverage/bounds/confidence: explicit, no renormalization.\\n12. Raw/final and override reconciliation: exact Decimal; stated cash cap and boundary tests only.\\n13. Complete-matrix classification/compounder criteria: NS/holds prevent designation.\\n14. Position-based liquidity: lot arithmetic completed; statistics/executable price still unresolved.\\n15. Strongest bear and thesis-break: explicit and falsifiable.\\n16. Common-version ranking/unfinished evidence: no investable ranking; gaps visibly unresolved.\\n\\nThe complete owned compact and full packages contain authored inputs and standard-library reconstruction code. Both regenerate the full canonical record, all failed paths, CSV ledger, score audit, calculations and report without original PDFs, network access or the other archive. A guarded clean rebuild deletes generated outputs **and validation/hash attestations**, then recreates them and checks against an external retained manifest. Source-safe packaging excludes downloaded PDFs/OCR/HTML, transfer helpers and private service identifiers. Technical reconstruction proves determinism and internal identities, not the economic truth of unverified issuer statements. Independent review and fresh Library-download tests were completed before the package was frozen.\\n\"","\"number\"","\"content_markdown\"","\"Executive assessment\"","\"\\n**Hold, unresolved accounting, funding and tradability.** This is the ethnic-fashion retailer Riyaasat Lifestyle Limited, not a life insurer. The supplied label “Riyaasat Life” resolves to ISIN INE0KYI01012, BSE SME 544804, symbol RIYAASAT. No corporate name change was found: it was incorporated under its current name on 23 October 2021. The CIN prefix changed from unlisted U to listed L, not the business identity. [RHP, printed 199; SECURITY]\\n\\nThere is a genuine operating business and revenue growth, but the evidence does not establish a high-quality, self-financing compounder. Revenue rose from ₹20.9349cr in FY23 to ₹33.3746cr in FY26, a comparable three-year CAGR of **16.82%**. FY26 inventory was ₹41.0462cr, larger than annual revenue, with a physical-verification limitation and inventory maintained outside the main accounting software. Bank statements have not reconciled to books in two successive audits. Four-year disclosed cumulative CFO/PAT is **-0.359**, not an average of annual ratios. [RHP, printed 230–233; AR26_BSE, PDF60–61,69–71,96–97]\\n\\nThe listing raised ₹30.19728cr gross, not all cash still available in October. A subsequent ₹15.5cr property purchase and revised store commitments materially change the investment case. Current cash, available facilities and a reliable BSE trading history remain unverified. An apparently moderate **20.35×** current-share FY26 P/E therefore is not a margin-of-safety conclusion. No trade, position allocation or favorable investment ranking is recommended.\\n\\n**Decisive variable:** can independently verified inventory turn into cash without material markdowns while the expanded store plan is funded and generates returns above its cost of capital? The strongest competing interpretation is that inventory and owned property are investment ahead of profitable growth. That interpretation needs evidence, not an EBITDA extrapolation.\\n\\n\"","\"Research header, gates and confidence\"","\"\\n- Information cutoff: **20 October 2026, Asia/Kolkata**. Market reference: **19 October 2026**. Later research retrieval dates do not extend the information cutoff.\\n- Scope: standalone company. FY26 directors report says no subsidiary, joint venture or associate company. Related-party “associate concerns” are not assumed to be equity-accounted investments.\\n- Accounting: Indian AS, Schedule III Division I, not IndAS. Store rent is an operating expense; do not graft lease-adjusted peer EBITDA multiples onto these accounts.\\n- Price: **₹98.4, rounded secondary 19 October close**, not a verified executable BSE price. Issued shares **10,744,478**, lot **1,200**, authorized shares **15,500,000**. The dated NSE interoperability master verifies security metadata, not an NSE listing or BSE turnover.\\n- Reference position: ₹5 lakh. Confidence: identity high; historical revenue medium-high; inventory/earnings and current funding low; execution low.\\n\\n| Gate | Assessment | Evidence and required resolution |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| G1 Governance | Conditional | Family control, old related-party exposure and disclosure errors; no established fraud/default. Obtain corrected notes and succession/RPT schedules. |\\n| G2 Accounting | **Hold, unresolved** | FY26 inventory-verification limitation; repeated bank-versus-books mismatches; historical audit-trail weakness. Reconcile independently. |\\n| G3 Funding | **Hold, unresolved** | Very low audited cash, high debt, property and expanded store commitments; October facilities and cash unavailable. A simulation failure is not evidence of an actual default. |\\n| G4 Tradability | **Hold, unresolved** | 20/60-session BSE traded-value statistics, current spread/depth and surveillance status unavailable. Missing bars are not zero-trade observations. |\\n\\nThe audit reports’ unmodified main/IFC opinions are positive contrary evidence, not a reason to ignore their annexures. No actual G1 or G3 failure override is imposed merely because a conservative forward model fails.\\n\\n\"","\"Canonical scorecard and scoring audit\"","\"\\n| Score | Result | Coverage | Known contribution | Uncapped bounds | Effective bounds |\\n|---|---|---:|---:|---|---|\\n|BQS|NS|80.00%|3.4600|3.66–5.46|3.66–5.46|\\n|GQS|NS|70.00%|4.0000|4.30–7.00|4.30–5.00|\\n|IAS|NS|0.00%|0.0000|1.00–10.00|1.00–10.00|\\n\\n| Factor | Score | Core reasoning | Contrary evidence / uncertainty |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n| GOV | 4 | Concentrated family control; recurring reporting/control weaknesses. | Unmodified opinions, no established fraud/default; shareholder approval for changed objects. |\\n| MOAT | 4 | Local design/customization capability, but six-store scale, regional dependence and nonexclusive supply. | Premium mix and gross economics suggest a local franchise; no audited repeat-cohort advantage. |\\n| REV | 7 | 16.82% three-year nominal organic revenue CAGR; no acquired operating company identified. | Unit/price/SSSG decomposition ND; new store and short history matter. |\\n| DUR | 5 | Recurring wedding/festival use and broad men/women assortment. | Discretionary demand, fashion changes and geographic concentration. |\\n| CAP | NS | Investment base sharply expanded; property advances retained in invested capital. | New-capital cohort returns and inventory validity are insufficiently established. |\\n| CASH | 2 | Persistent weak cumulative conversion before capex; inventory consumes funds. | FY25 positive CFO, but capital-advance/tax classifications affect its quality. |\\n| BAL | NS | ₹41.36cr audited debt versus ₹0.128cr cash; major later commitments. | IPO provided capital; no default reported, but current usable liquidity ND. |\\n| EXEC | 4 | Mumbai added; then IPO locations, comparable cost and deadline changed. | Approved changes may be economically sensible, but delivery/returns not yet demonstrated. |\\n| EARN | NS | Core EBITDA is reconstructed, not copied from inconsistent KPI definitions. | Inventory limitation prevents a reliable earnings-quality score. |\\n| R | NS | No verified funded base-case IRR. | Conditional simulations show requirements, not actual financing availability. |\\n| D | NS | Inventory haircut, supplier withdrawal and property illiquidity can impair equity. | Recoverable liquidation values and probabilities ND. |\\n| P | NS | Apparent P/E is measurable conditionally; current intrinsic value is not. | Accounting/funding uncertainty defeats a numeric margin-of-safety claim. |\\n| L | NS | Position/lot defined, required trading-value observations absent. | Listing and metadata do not establish executable volume. |\\n\\nThe actual v3 weights are used exactly. BQS: GOV20%, MOAT20%, REV12%, DUR10%, CAP12%, CASH10%, BAL8%, EXEC8%. GQS: REV40%, DUR20%, EARN15%, CAP15%, CASH10%. IAS: final unrounded BQS25%, R35%, D20%, P10%, L10%. No missing factor is replaced with 5, and no weight is renormalized. Bound = known weighted contribution plus missing weight × [1,10]. Persistent CASH2 caps GQS at5, including its effective upper bound; it does **not** convert NS to a score. GOV3 and proven-G3-failure boundary tests are retained separately. Decimal arithmetic and half-up display rounding are used throughout. Each canonical factor’s supporting facts, contrary fact, source IDs and confidence are in `factor_evidence.json`.\\n\\nTwo uncertain scored factors are tested at ±1. GOV3/GOV5 boundary cases are retained; the severe-governance cap in the GOV3 case is hypothetical, not the current GOV4 judgment. Separately, MOAT3 lowers BQS raw bounds to 3.46–5.26, while MOAT5 raises them to 3.86–5.66. MOAT has no direct GQS weight, so GQS is unchanged. IAS remains NS in both cases; its informative nested bounds shift by −0.05 and +0.05 respectively through the 25% BQS weight. These sensitivities do not fill missing factors or establish investability.\\n\\n\"","\"Business model and economic anatomy\"","\"\\nRiyaasat designs, manufactures through its production ecosystem, sources and retails ethnic apparel for men and women, including sherwanis, lehenga choli, Indo-western outfits, kurtas and Jodhpuris. The customer is primarily an occasionwear buyer, not a contracted industrial customer. Customization, local taste, assortment and the in-store experience matter. Merchandise ownership, physical stores and fashion obsolescence make this a cash-intensive retail model, not an asset-light brand royalty stream. [RHP, business section 151–198]\\n\\nManufacturing represented about 35.06% of FY25 revenue and trading 64.94%; the January 2026 mix moved to roughly 25.58% manufacturing. This is not pure manufacturing growth. FY25’s five leading categories represented 74.09% of sales: sherwani 19.84%, lehenga choli 19.19%, Indo-western 16.02%, kurta 12.13% and Jodhpuri6.91%. A changing mix can alter margins without demonstrating price increases. B2B selling through PSL Retail/Pernia’s Pop-Up is disclosed, but no audited channel-profit or repeat-customer cohort is available. [RHP]\\n\\nThe disclosed six stores total about 25,900sqft: four Ahmedabad locations, Vadodara and owned Santacruz Mumbai. Five stores were present in FY23–FY25; Mumbai made six in FY26. RHP milestone language and store-opening tables are not perfectly aligned on initial timing, so exact per-store cohorts are not manufactured. Gujarat accounted for 96.59% of FY25 revenue and 67.13% of the January 2026 period; Maharashtra and other channels explain much of the mix shift. No export-led scaling thesis is established.\\n\\nDirect materials/traded merchandise plus separately disclosed manufacturing expenses are combined into a broad COGS proxy. The naïve FY26 sales less material/purchases/inventory-change calculation overstates gross margin because job work, labour and consumables sit in other expenses. The broad FY26 COGS proxy is ₹10.2653cr and gross margin about 69.24%, still subject to payroll-allocation and inventory-valuation limits. Rent of ₹3.1424cr is already expensed.\\n\\n\"","\"Competitive position and named economic peers\"","\"\\nThe local franchise is credible, but durable pricing power is not demonstrated by one year’s high accounting margin. There are no exclusive supplier arrangements, and expansion outside familiar western markets requires different demand knowledge. Disclosed supplier-concentration table headings and percentage denominators are inconsistent: the stated percentages cannot simply be treated as percentages of revenue. No precise supplier-share claim is inferred from that mislabeled denominator. [RHP, printed 24–25]\\n\\n| Peer | FY26 revenue | FY26 PAT | Raw CFO/PAT | 19 Oct price | P/E |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|Vedant Fashions Limited|1435.50|375.50|1.28|509.85|32.99|\\n|Go Fashion (India) Limited|838.01|59.18|2.78|265.40|23.59|\\n|Sai Silks (Kalamandir) Limited|1653.67|140.92|2.29|72.54|7.89|\\n\\nAll peer prices above are from the official **19 October 2026 NSE bhavcopy**, and FY26 operating facts come from primary reports, not an aggregator comparison. [PEERMARKET; VEDANT26; GO26; SAI26]\\n\\n- **Vedant Fashions:** closest weddingwear/men’s occasionwear brand comparison, but 669 EBOs and franchise/wholesale economics differ sharply. FY26 reported revenue grew 3.5%, retail customer sales 6.1%, SSSG2.7%. Raw CFO/PAT is about 128%, not the presentation’s adjusted 98% cash-conversion metric. Lease principal plus interest was ₹192.4cr and capex ₹12.3cr. Large financial assets, brand reach and different capital ownership mean its multiple is not transferable.\\n- **Go Fashion:** useful store/inventory execution comparator, not a weddingwear clone. Revenue declined, PAT fell and cash-flow optics are affected by IndAS leases. CFO₹164.7048cr less lease cash ₹139.3933cr and capex ₹39.1573cr leaves negative cash. The primary FY26 report has 52,595,984 post-buyback shares; the dated exchange security master still carries 54,008,984. Both denominator valuations are retained rather than silently adopting a stale master. Weighted EPS is not used for current market cap.\\n- **Sai Silks:** ethnic-fashion retail with 81 stores and 3% SSSG, but different regional, women’s and store-size mix. ₹322.52cr CFO benefited from a₹137.14cr payable increase. Subtract₹50.64cr capex and ₹43.90cr lease principal; finance cash ₹30.98cr is separately disclosed. Do not compare this principal-only lease deduction directly with peers’ all-in lease cash without that adjustment.\\n\\nThese primary peers show that same-store growth can be far below new-store growth, inventory ties up cash, and rent accounting matters. A simple average P/E or EV/EBITDA does not establish Riyaasat fair value. FY26 accounts are the common valuation earnings denominator, explicitly not an overlapping TTM or fabricated half-year cash-flow series. Current-year results, if available for individual peers, are not silently spliced into this common FY26 comparison.\\n\\n\"","6","\"Revenue quality and organic growth\"","\"\\n| Year | Revenue | Core EBITDA | PAT | CFO | CFO/PAT | Gross debt | Inventory |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n|FY23|20.93|1.81|1.32|-1.12|-0.85|1.02|5.27|\\n|FY24|22.88|5.35|4.08|-2.73|-0.67|4.74|14.97|\\n|FY25|24.80|6.42|4.87|1.49|0.31|8.87|21.40|\\n|FY26|33.37|10.19|5.19|-3.19|-0.61|41.36|41.05|\\n\\nAmounts are ₹crore. FY23–FY25 use the RHP restated series; FY26 uses the original annual report. This is a disclosed mixed-vintage bridge, not a fully restated four-year series. Original FY22–FY25 figures and subsequent comparatives remain separately available in inputs.\\n\\nFY24 revenue growth was 9.27%, FY25 8.43%, FY26 34.55%. The three-year endpoint CAGR is 16.82%, qualifying for REV7 without an upward discretionary adjustment. FY22 revenue ₹4.364602cr covers the incorporation stub, so a headline FY22–FY26 “four-year CAGR” would be misleading. The business is young, but a small absolute base is not itself grounds to punish growth mechanically.\\n\\nNo acquired operating business explains the expansion identified here. A property purchase is capital investment, not acquired revenue. Store openings are organic expansion, but not same-store growth. Price, units, customer counts, realized discount rates and audited SSSG are unavailable. Therefore nominal revenue growth is not called real volume growth or pricing power. Management’s existing-store growth expectations and contribution from new stores remain guidance, not independent proof. Strong FY26 revenue alongside inventory growth of roughly 92% raises a cash-demand question rather than confirming high-quality demand.\\n\\n\"","\"Growth, reinvestment and incremental capital\"","\"\\nThe economic capital bridge is central. FY25 PPE was only ₹0.2927cr, but another ₹6.3775cr property advance was already in other noncurrent assets. Ignoring it would exaggerate subsequent returns. FY26 PPE reached ₹31.8852cr, inventory ₹41.0462cr and gross debt ₹41.3623cr. Gross PPE cash ₹32.7408cr is not the full net investing outflow: other noncurrent assets released ₹6.2336cr, giving net investing ₹26.5072cr. FY25’s₹4.8cr capital advance received in other current liabilities went tozero and its cash movement is not added a second time as capex. [RHP; AR26_BSE]\\n\\n`financial_calculations.json` shows financing capital (equity + debt − cash), a broad operating-capital reconstruction, their residual, normalized NOPAT at25% tax, average-capital returns, one-year changes and a three-elapsed-year FY23end→FY26end change. Broad operating capital includes identified property advances, supplier advances and deposits, excludes identified tax/prepaidIPO assets, and removes capital-advance financing from operating liabilities. Some earlier tax/advance splits remain inseparable, so residuals are visible and these are analytical proxies, not audited cohort ROIC.\\n\\nThe one-year incremental NOPAT/incremental operating-capital proxy is **6.13%**, and the three-year endpoint proxy **9.62%**. Both are timing-sensitive; FY26’s large investments were not productive for a full year, inventory is not independently validated, and original/restated vintages differ. A one-year-lagged FY26 NOPAT/FY25 capital diagnostic is also calculated, explicitly flagged as excluding much of the new investment and therefore potentially overstating returns. Commissioned-store cohort ROIC is ND because in-service dates and store-level NOPAT are unavailable. For a risky SME retailer, the analyst cost-of-capital range used here is 14%–18%, not an observed market quotation. Both the 6.13% one-year and 9.62% three-year proxies are below that range, although timing and reliability limitations prevent a definitive cohort-return conclusion. The full five-year incremental-return window is ND. Those limitations justify CAP NS rather than awarding a high return score from a small old capital denominator or treating negative CFO as merely “growth capex.”\\n\\n### Post-IPO capital plan, on a comparable basis\\n\\nOriginal four-store fitout and deposits totaled ₹13.5503cr, versus ₹12.47cr IPO allocation. The original rent schedule was not included in that capex total. September’s revised Mumbai and Ahmedabad headline table includes Ahmedabad annual rent ₹1.5732cr, so adding headline totals directly would double count rent already in modeled operating margins.\\n\\nAfter removing that annual rent, revised fitout is ₹17.2973cr and new deposits ₹1.9422cr, together ₹19.2395cr. Separately, the new Santacruz property cost ₹15.5cr. The fitout/deposit plan alone exceeds the unchanged IPO store allocation by ₹6.7695cr; including the additional property makes the incremental cash requirement much larger. This is not proof of current insolvency, but it makes current funding verification indispensable. [POSTAL; PROPERTY]\\n\\nOriginal Surat premises fell through after the owner cancelled on 15 July. Mumbai expansion replaces the original store location concept; Ahmedabad replaces Surat, while Hyderabad/Bengaluru remain. Shareholders approved the changes on 1 October, with results 3 October. The deadline moved to twelve months from approval, versus the original FY27 rollout. Sale certificate 22 September received 30 September supports a property event, not an independently confirmed cash-payment date; an earlier-payment sensitivity is retained.\\n\\n\"","8","\"Management, governance and execution audit\"","\"\\nThe Galiya family’s7,885,428 promoter shares are about 73.39% of post-IPO issued shares. The postal ballot’s valid votes were 7,924,478, dominated by promoters, with 39,050 public votes and no votes against. Legal approval is relevant, but not independent commercial validation. Current promoter encumbrance and a comprehensive post-listing dilutive-instrument register are ND; absence of a new filing in this research is not proof of no pledge or no future dilution. [VOTE; RHP]\\n\\nExecution evidence is mixed: five stores sustained through FY25, a sixth Mumbai store and higher FY26 revenue are positive. Soon after listing, two store choices, comparable budget and completion timetable changed. The company’s statement in the RHP that it had no previous time/cost overrun describes that earlier record; it does not erase later scope escalation. The new owned-property plan may reduce rent or improve location quality, but those benefits need occupancy, throughput, cash-return and implementation evidence.\\n\\nOriginal FY23 accounts show ₹1.74cr advanced to Dhagaa, a firm in which all directors were partners, about 79% of original equity. The loan lacked a stipulated repayment/interest schedule. Subsequent related-party tables contain classification/summation inconsistencies. These are material governance questions, but no inference of siphoning is asserted without settlement and bank evidence. [AR2023, PDF22,38; RHP RPTnotes]\\n\\nOriginal FY26 directors remuneration ₹0.46cr, rent/commission ₹0.664cr, inconsistent KMP totals and CSR disclosure differences require reconciliation. The corporate-information page calls Sobhanaben independent while substantive disclosures identify her as the MD’s mother and WTD’s spouse. Treat the relationship as controlling evidence, not the erroneous label. Proper independents elsewhere in the governance section are not thereby assumed absent. [AR26_BSE]\\n\\nThe RHP records Arpan Chudgar’s6 June 2023 resignation for other assignments and successor appointment 3 July effective 1 July. Yet Arpan’s original FY23 opinion is dated 29 August 2023. The chronology needs original appointment/resignation filings. “No resignation during FY26” is not “no historical auditor resignation.” No causal accusation is inferred from the dates.\\n\\n\"","9","\"Financial forensics and full audit sequence\"","\"\\nOriginal FY22–FY26 audits and all available CARO/IFC annexures were read. FY25 image-only audit page16 and the audit-trail paragraph were visually checked. The complete source-specific findings are retained in `audit_evidence.json`.\\n\\n- **FY22:** Arpan, unmodified; CARO/IFC stated exempt. The unusual going-concern emphasis says plans to improve but explicitly no uncertainty. It is not a material-uncertainty opinion.\\n- **FY23:** Arpan, unmodified main/IFC; inventory verification positive; Dhagaa on-demand/unfixed-term exposure. CARO’s no-lender-borrowing wording conflicts with the promoter-relative debt note. Same qualified-in-meaning, not modified-opinion, going-concern emphasis.\\n- **FY24:** Abhishek, unmodified main/IFC; explicit absence of audit-trail software in Other Matter. Inventory verification positive. CARO loan/no-loan language is internally inconsistent. No default/fraud/resignation reported.\\n- **FY25:** unmodified main/IFC; bank statements disagree with books. Audit-trail paragraph simultaneously says software not used and that the feature operated, unresolved contradictory wording. Inventory verification still positive.\\n- **FY26:** unmodified main/IFC and positive audit-trail paragraph, **but inventory verification impracticable**, bank/books mismatch continues, and inventory is outside Tally Prime. No reported fraud/default/wilful default/current-year auditor resignation; disputed income-tax ₹1.1812cr; no one-year liability-payment material uncertainty identified, which is not a future-funding guarantee.\\n\\n### Reconciliation rather than superficial red flags\\n\\n1. FY26 CFO is ₹−3.1932cr. The line labeled cash generated from operations is actually a working-capital subtotal. Adding₹10.4943cr operating profit and deducting ₹1.1073cr tax reconciles the final CFO within source rounding. This is a presentation defect, not evidence that final CFO fails arithmetic.\\n2. Original FY24 CFO₹−3.463378cr, FY25 comparative ₹−2.4215cr and RHP restated ₹−2.7276cr are different vintages. Original FY23 CFO₹−1.427597cr differs from restated ₹−1.1239cr. Original and comparative AR/AP balances also changed. All are preserved, not silently spliced.\\n3. Original FY24 investing subtotal incorrectly includes CFO. The cash roll works with actual PPE cash ₹0.328577cr, not the cumulative displayed investing number. This historic labeling problem is distinct from the FY26 subtotal issue.\\n4. CARO’s IPO count 2,702,400 is the net offer. Adding146,400 market-maker shares gives 2,848,800 fresh shares; ×₹106 gives ₹30.19728cr. No ₹3,020lakh proceeds is inferred from the smaller count.\\n5. The malformed note 39 intermediary wording contradicts the main management representation and is interrupted by an IPO table. Preserve it as an unresolved document issue, not a proven routing transaction.\\n6. FY26 inventory’s10%/20% haircuts would be ₹4.10462/₹8.20924cr before tax, around 22.5%/44.9% of audited equity. These are sensitivities, not estimates that those losses occurred.\\n7. Receivables are much smaller than inventory; reported net ₹0.8762cr is mainly less than six months old. This is contrary evidence to a generalized claim of all sales being uncollected. Supplier funding is substantial: payables ₹14.5673cr, including ₹2.0754cr MSME, mostly under one year. Neither assertion proves inventory value.\\n8. FY26 finance cost ₹3.0759cr includes interest ₹2.5611cr, bank charges ₹0.0414cr and other borrowing cost ₹0.4735cr. Core EBITDA adds back all finance cost and depreciation then removes other income. An interest-only addback is not a comparable EBITDA definition. A separate normalized PAT/current-share EPS bridge removes other income and applies 25% tax to core PBT. It does not invent an inventory adjustment; EARN stays NS.\\n\\nCumulative CFO/PAT uses sums, not averages. CFO is shown both before and after financing interest. Big PPE capex explains weak FCF, not negative CFO. Inventory absorbed ₹19.6435cr while suppliers provided ₹10.4128cr; advances absorbed another ₹1.7252cr. Opening current-tax provision ₹2.1289cr is a separate claim, not discharged merely by paying tax on future profits. The cash model pays it gross; excluded advance-tax credit ₹0.1961cr is not silently netted or valued.\\n\\n\"","10","\"Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\"","\"\\nAt the secondary ₹98.4 reference and 10,744,478 issued shares, conditional equity market cap is **₹105.73cr**. FY26 current-share EPS is **₹4.8342**, so P/E is **20.35×**. Using reported weighted-average EPS₹6.58 would overstate earnings per current share after the IPO. March debt/cash combined with October market cap produces a stale-balance EV diagnostic only; it is **not** current EV. Normalized FY26 PAT is ₹4.47225cr, current-share normalized EPS₹4.16237 and conditional normalized P/E **23.64×**. This removes ₹0.3071cr other income and applies 25% tax to ₹5.9630cr core PBT; no speculative inventory adjustment is included. Original FY25 PAT₹4.7824cr versus RHP restated ₹4.8664cr differs by ₹0.0840cr prior-year tax and is retained as a vintage bridge. The modeled term/CC components sum ₹41.3624cr versus reported debt ₹41.3623cr, a₹0.0001cr source rounding residual, not hidden financing. Gross IPO proceeds cannot be both added to entry cash and spent on projects. [AR26_BSE; SECURITY; PRICE]\\n\\n### Cash model and funding policy\\n\\nThe model starts 31 March 2026, not with an invented healthy October balance. It rolls dated sales, collections, supplier payments, inventory/receivables/payables, other operating capital, rent-inclusive EBITDA, tax, maintenance/growth capex, property, fitout/deposits, interest, debt principal and cash. It checks cash **before late collections** as well as closing cash. The reduced operating model opens with net assets of ₹17.8382cr, not a claim to restate audited equity of ₹18.2762cr: excluded advance-tax asset ₹0.1961cr, prepaid IPO asset ₹0.1826cr, DTA ₹0.0592cr and debt-component rounding ₹0.0001cr reconcile the difference. Prepaid IPO cost is recovered once in the fee bridge; other excluded tax assets receive no invented cash recovery. Initial restricted IPO balances are zero until 1 July. Net issue cash is allocated to store/WC/GCP pools and released only against matched uses. Prior-paid IPO fees are reimbursed once. The full fee budget is deducted at listing as a conservative early-payment/reservation assumption; the postal notice’s ₹0.9236cr unspent fee allocation is not treated as free project cash. Full reported WC/GCP use is enforced on1 September: unmatched restricted amounts are released against an equal explicit unreconciled-use outflow, leaving no spendable cash. The residual is tracked as memorandum deployed capital, not an assertion of an audited recoverable asset, and receives no separate terminal recovery. Its inclusion in capital employed prevents ignoring the cash already deployed. This conservative diagnostic may overlap with unidentified future operating balances; current accounts are required to resolve the allocation, not a hidden cash plug.\\n\\nOrdinary bear/base/bull allow **zero unverified new credit**. A later equity issue cannot repair an earlier pre-entry failure. Separate diagnostics assume explicitly uncommitted, capped ₹20cr, ₹25cr and ₹30cr pre-entry bridges,2% fee, scenario interest and full repayment 19 October 2028. These diagnostics do not establish that a lender offered such terms. The ₹20cr and ₹25cr cases still fail before entry at the property payment and cannot be repaired by entry equity. The separately labeled ₹30cr cases exercise later liquidity and terminal logic. The ordinary, ₹20cr and ₹25cr failed paths all remain intact.\\n\\nEntry rescue issues only whole shares, at 30% discount with 5% issue friction, bounded by 15,500,000 authorized less 10,744,478 existing = **4,755,522 shares**. Each feasible minimum is tested against the adjacent failing share. The authorization ceiling is not a guarantee of issuance approval or subscription. Lower entry prices also lower rescue proceeds, so a nominal cheap-price hurdle cannot be accepted without re-solving dilution and liquidity.\\n\\nThe table and root tests below use the separate ₹30cr diagnostic, not the actual funding case. Base forward inputs are ₹45cr entry revenue run-rate,18% annual growth,25% EBITDA after rent, inventory gradually falling to 75% of annual sales,30% payable/sales intensity,10 debtor days,13% interest and 25% normalized cash tax. Bear uses lower growth/margin and slower inventory conversion; bull uses 28% growth,30% margin and inventory 50% of sales. These are analytical assumptions, not management guidance. Principal uses disclosed first-year maturity and a finite assumed runoff; CC is repaid rather than automatically renewed. Exact contracts and current renewals remain ND.\\n\\n| Case | Ordinary | ₹30cr bridge + reference-price rescue | ₹30cr bridge + ₹200-price diagnostic | ₹30cr bridge + ₹300-price diagnostic |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n|bear|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-45.84% IRR; 3802838 rescue shares|\\n|base|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|-26.01% IRR; 3187768 rescue shares|\\n|bull|CASH_BREACH|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-3.46% IRR; 4657910 rescue shares|-9.06% IRR; 3105274 rescue shares|\\n\\n\\nThe ₹200 and ₹300 columns are deliberately **not** price targets. They test whether a higher-priced, same-authorized-share-cap fundraising can finance the model and what that does to the original investor’s return. A higher financing price can make the cash path feasible while making the shareholder return unattractive. A conditional funded path is not a favorable actual-funding conclusion.\\n\\n### Earned terminal value, not an arbitrary exit P/E\\n\\nThe horizon ends 19 October 2031, followed by a full twelve-month operating/cash continuation. Terminal ROIC is capped at the lower of an explicit ceiling and actual modeled NOPAT/invested capital, excluding cash; a separate one-month revenue cash reserve is also funded. Reinvestment is the greater of the physical capital-intensity requirement and NOPAT×g/ROIC. Reserve growth is separately retained and deducted from FCFF. Value = sustainable FCFF/(WACC−g), with positive spread and growth-return constraints. No terminal value is admitted unless the forward business meets intraperiod liquidity, reserve and no-new-financing self-funding tests.\\n\\nEnterprise value is bridged to common equity by debt/senior claims and only cash above the required operating reserve. Restricted funds are excluded. Residual debt service is not deducted twice through both a reserve and EV-to-equity bridge. No dividends are forecast, so retained cash is not also counted as distributed cash. Cash failures suppress ordinary IRR, even if an attractive suffix could be drawn after the failure.\\n\\n### Hurdles and sensitivities\\n\\nThe complete model retains **341 unique losslessly deduplicated paths** and all requested references, including failed ordinary paths, rescue bounds, adjacent failures, root trials, feasibility boundaries and forward schedules. It includes 12%/15%/18% joint price/dilution hurdles; reverse growth;5×/10× tests; growth-margin and WACC-terminal-growth grids; AR/inventory/payables; interest; collection timing; fitout overrun; six-month delay; earlier property payment; early CC repayment; inventory markdown; contingent-tax settlement and an explicitly optimistic productivity case. Exact inputs and root domains are visible.\\n\\n| Case | Hurdle | Joint entry-price result, domain ₹20–₹500 | Funding boundary |\\n|---|---:|---|---|\\n|base|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|base|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|201.0986–201.1047|\\n|bull|12.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|15.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n|bull|18.00%|NO_ADMISSIBLE_SAMPLED_BRACKET|195.8923–195.8954|\\n\\n\\nAll six joint-price tests found no admissible return-hurdle bracket within ₹20–₹500. Funding first becomes feasible around ₹201.0986–201.1047 in the base and ₹195.8923–195.8954 in the bull diagnostic, but the funded shareholder returns do not meet 12%/15%/18%. These are fundraising feasibility boundaries, not buy prices. Reverse growth at the ₹98.4 reference found no admissible sampled hurdle bracket over 0%–80% annual growth, because bounded funding fails. The 5×/10× search over 0%–150% growth likewise establishes neither outcome; it is not a claim that every mathematically possible future is disproven.\\n\\n| Base sensitivity, separate ₹30cr bridge | Reference ₹98.4 | At ₹200: conditional IRR / rescue shares |\\n|---|---|---|\\n|margin_minus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|margin_plus5pp|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-15.09% / 4310194|\\n|inventory_50pct_sales|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-16.78% / 4173660|\\n|rate18pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|fitout_overrun25pct|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n|optimistic_productivity|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|-6.07% / 3109156|\\n|contingent_tax_paid|BOUNDED_RESCUE_INFEASIBLE|CASH_BREACH|\\n\\n\\nAt the reference price, even the displayed optimistic productivity case remains unfunded under the share cap. At₹200, lower inventory intensity improves the diagnostic but does not deliver the hurdle; a25% fitout overrun increases funding strain and 18% borrowing cost defeats bounded rescue.\\n\\nRoot searches are bounded sampled tests, not global proofs. An unfunded point is not assigned a negative IRR for interpolation. No root is interpolated across a funding discontinuity. Fixed-funding discount-back prices are shown only as diagnostics and retested through the joint funding framework. Execution-cost sensitivities use both entry and exit friction at 0%,1% and 3%. EV/NOPAT multiple-normalization tests are secondary sensitivities, not P/E tests of debt-free earnings; named peers do not justify a chosen terminal multiple. Terminal-value dependency is explicit because no interim shareholder cash distributions are assumed. A separate exact attribution identity decomposes price change into growth in normalized common-earnings proxy, share-count dilution and implied equity-multiple change. It is not a new valuation assumption; revenue scale is separately labeled.\\n\\n\"","11","\"Strongest bear case and thesis-break variables\"","\"\\nThe strongest bear case is not simply “small company.” It is that accounting profits precede cash by a very long period, fashion inventory is overstated or slow moving, suppliers/banks stop extending credit, and owned property plus expanding stores consume the IPO proceeds before new stores earn acceptable returns. A modest inventory impairment can absorb a large share of reported equity; a forced sale of merchandise or property may recover much less than book value. Debt-service timing then matters more than the exit multiple.\\n\\nThis case is falsifiable. Independently reconciled stock counts/aging/NRV, audited cash conversion, bank-book reconciliation and adequate committed facilities would weaken it. Mere revenue growth with still faster inventory growth would not. The model tests inventory book writeoff separately from cash replacement and lost-growth/margin stresses: a noncash markdown alone is not incorrectly modeled as an immediate cash payment, and accounting writeoff does not create sale proceeds.\\n\\nThesis-break indicators: inventory write-downs or unexplained inventory-age growth; continued bank mismatch; unfunded capex commitments; supplier/CC withdrawal; a material RPT increase without settlement; repeated unexplained share or fund-use discrepancies. None is represented as having occurred beyond the documented evidence.\\n\\n\"","12","\"Five-times/ten-times and compounder test\"","\"\\nFive-times in five years requires 37.97% annual shareholder compounding; ten-times requires 58.49%. Revenue growth alone cannot produce those returns if additional working capital, fixed assets, financing costs and share dilution absorb the gains. The reverse paths solve for growth over a bounded domain while retaining all cash failures. No “5× potential” is awarded merely because a tiny initial base could mathematically expand.\\n\\nRiyaasat does not presently meet the v3 compounder test: real volume/pricing growth is not isolated, moat evidence is modest, incremental capital returns are unproven, cumulative cash conversion is weak, and critical accounting/funding/tradability gates remain open. Successful store expansion may eventually change that conclusion, but a turnaround in cash quality is a hypothesis, not a designation.\\n\\n\"","13","\"Monitoring and information needed\"","\"\\n| Priority | Evidence | What would change the assessment |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Independently verified SKU stock, age, NRV and physical reconciliation | Determines inventory and earnings reliability; prerequisite for G2 release. |\\n| 1 | Bank quarterly/monthly reconciliations, sanction letters, utilization, covenants and exact maturity ladder | Determines usable headroom and G3; no reliance on gross sanctioned limits. |\\n| 1 |31 March→19 October cash roll, property payment schedule and all IPO-bank/utilization statements | Replaces pre-entry assumptions; identifies committed versus uncommitted funding. |\\n| 2 | Store-level revenue, area, opening dates, contribution after rent and cash invested | Tests organic growth, payback and incremental ROIC. |\\n| 2 | Corrected audit-trail, intermediary, KMP/CSR and RPT schedules; auditor succession filings | Resolves disclosure/control concerns proportionately. |\\n| 2 | Current share/option/warrant and encumbrance register | Confirms dilution and governance exposures. |\\n| 1 before execution | Verified BSE20/60-session complete calendar/value data, current lot/segment, spread/depth and restrictions | Allows L scoring and position-size execution test. |\\n\\nAt₹98.4 one 1,200-share lot costs ₹118,080 before fees; four lots ₹472,320 and five ₹590,400. The₹5lakh reference cannot be treated as an exactly divisible order. Entry/exit days at 2%/5%/10% participation are ND until both 20/60 means and medians are established, with genuine zero-trade days retained. Initial ten-day trade-for-trade arrangements do not establish October status. No order-book liquidity or price-band escape is assumed.\\n\\n\"","14","\"Final classification and update discipline\"","\"\\n**Hold, unresolved. BQS NS, GQS NS (cap 5), IAS NS.** The score matrix cannot assign a numerical quality/attractiveness quadrant with missing canonical factors, and critical gates override an attractive-looking price. No compounder, multibagger or investable ranking designation is made. This is not an evidenced fraud/default “Avoid” classification, either.\\n\\nUpdate log,20 October cutoff: the RHP-only view is superseded by original FY22–FY26 audit reading and post-IPO project disclosures. Revenue evidence supports REV7; persistent cash weakness supports CASH2 and the GQS cap. CAP/EARN/BAL and all investment-attractiveness factors remain NS because material evidence is missing. Original-versus-restated cash figures, bank mismatch recurrence and inventory-system limitations are retained rather than overwritten. These are evidence/methodology clarifications, not a claim that the market price itself changed.\\n\\nNo batch winner or overall portfolio exit count is inferred. Rankings, if later requested, must use a common framework version, complete universe, explicit tie rules and gate eligibility. SME status remains distinct from the previously researched main-board universe.\\n\\n\"","15","\"Sources, assumptions, recovery and final quality check\"","\"\\nSource IDs link to primary URLs, retrieval hashes and locations in `source_register.json`. Key source links: [RHP](https://aibi.org.in/Current_DOC/26270048.pdf), [issuer annual-report archive](https://www.riyaasatlifestyle.in/pages/annual-report), [FY26 exchange filing](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/95e91031-ad8c-484b-824d-eb4463efff51.pdf), [postal notice](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0780/2116/5367/files/Postal_Ballot_Notice.pdf?v=1788248084), [property disclosure](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/eb3a0341-f571-481c-8e13-f60b4824c89a.pdf), [voting result](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/5ad21c51-aa55-40a6-8664-c729f1b9beaa.pdf). Exchange documents retrieved via a public mirror retain the primary and mirror URLs plus exact byte hash. Peer sources and selected official market rows are retained separately.\\n\\nFactual extracts, management statements and analyst assumptions are distinct. All money inputs are crore unless expressly stated; source FY22–FY24 originals use thousands, RHP and FY25–FY26 use lakh, Vedant uses million. No overlapping TTM, invented quarterly cash flow or post-cutoff financial result is used. Missing is not zero. This is original analysis, not redistribution of source PDFs/OCR.\\n\\n### All sixteen final v3 checks\\n\\n1. Correct issuer/security/scope and dated price: checked; secondary execution limitation explicit.\\n2. Material primary records read; guidance and assumptions labeled: checked, full audit sequence included.\\n3. Audit/RPT/funding/dilution reviewed: checked; unresolved gaps remain gates, not fabricated clearance.\\n4. Periods/units/no overlapping TTM: checked; original/restated vintages separate.\\n5. Revenue versus earnings/acquisitions/base effects: checked; price/volume ND and startup stub excluded.\\n6. CFO/PAT ratio of sums; CFO versus capex: checked.\\n7. Incremental numerator/denominator/timing: visible; proxies qualified, five-year window ND.\\n8. Normalized tax/leases/claims/dilution: explicit; opening tax paid, no unsupported netting.\\n9. Bear/base/bull cash and financing: full traces retained, failures suppress returns.\\n10. Canonical factors and 100% weights: independently asserted.\\n11. NS/coverage/bounds/confidence: explicit, no renormalization.\\n12. Raw/final and override reconciliation: exact Decimal; stated cash cap and boundary tests only.\\n13. Complete-matrix classification/compounder criteria: NS/holds prevent designation.\\n14. Position-based liquidity: lot arithmetic completed; statistics/executable price still unresolved.\\n15. Strongest bear and thesis-break: explicit and falsifiable.\\n16. Common-version ranking/unfinished evidence: no investable ranking; gaps visibly unresolved.\\n\\nThe complete owned compact and full packages contain authored inputs and standard-library reconstruction code. Both regenerate the full canonical record, all failed paths, CSV ledger, score audit, calculations and report without original PDFs, network access or the other archive. A guarded clean rebuild deletes generated outputs **and validation/hash attestations**, then recreates them and checks against an external retained manifest. Source-safe packaging excludes downloaded PDFs/OCR/HTML, transfer helpers and private service identifiers. Technical reconstruction proves determinism and internal identities, not the economic truth of unverified issuer statements. Independent review and fresh Library-download tests must close before the package is treated as frozen.\\n\"","\"\\nSource IDs link to primary URLs, retrieval hashes and locations in `source_register.json`. Key source links: [RHP](https://aibi.org.in/Current_DOC/26270048.pdf), [issuer annual-report archive](https://www.riyaasatlifestyle.in/pages/annual-report), [FY26 exchange filing](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/95e91031-ad8c-484b-824d-eb4463efff51.pdf), [postal notice](https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0780/2116/5367/files/Postal_Ballot_Notice.pdf?v=1788248084), [property disclosure](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/eb3a0341-f571-481c-8e13-f60b4824c89a.pdf), [voting result](https://www.bseindia.com/xml-data/corpfiling/AttachHis/5ad21c51-aa55-40a6-8664-c729f1b9beaa.pdf). Exchange documents retrieved via a public mirror retain the primary and mirror URLs plus exact byte hash. Peer sources and selected official market rows are retained separately.\\n\\nFactual extracts, management statements and analyst assumptions are distinct. All money inputs are crore unless expressly stated; source FY22–FY24 originals use thousands, RHP and FY25–FY26 use lakh, Vedant uses million. No overlapping TTM, invented quarterly cash flow or post-cutoff financial result is used. Missing is not zero. This is original analysis, not redistribution of source PDFs/OCR.\\n\\n### All sixteen final v3 checks\\n\\n1. Correct issuer/security/scope and dated price: checked; secondary execution limitation explicit.\\n2. Material primary records read; guidance and assumptions labeled: checked, full audit sequence included.\\n3. Audit/RPT/funding/dilution reviewed: checked; unresolved gaps remain gates, not fabricated clearance.\\n4. Periods/units/no overlapping TTM: checked; original/restated vintages separate.\\n5. Revenue versus earnings/acquisitions/base effects: checked; price/volume ND and startup stub excluded.\\n6. CFO/PAT ratio of sums; CFO versus capex: checked.\\n7. Incremental numerator/denominator/timing: visible; proxies qualified, five-year window ND.\\n8. Normalized tax/leases/claims/dilution: explicit; opening tax paid, no unsupported netting.\\n9. Bear/base/bull cash and financing: full traces retained, failures suppress returns.\\n10. Canonical factors and 100% weights: independently asserted.\\n11. NS/coverage/bounds/confidence: explicit, no renormalization.\\n12. Raw/final and override reconciliation: exact Decimal; stated cash cap and boundary tests only.\\n13. Complete-matrix classification/compounder criteria: NS/holds prevent designation.\\n14. Position-based liquidity: lot arithmetic completed; statistics/executable price still unresolved.\\n15. Strongest bear and thesis-break: explicit and falsifiable.\\n16. Common-version ranking/unfinished evidence: no investable ranking; gaps visibly unresolved.\\n\\nThe complete owned compact and full packages contain authored inputs and standard-library reconstruction code. Both regenerate the full canonical record, all failed paths, CSV ledger, score audit, calculations and report without original PDFs, network access or the other archive. A guarded clean rebuild deletes generated outputs **and validation/hash attestations**, then recreates them and checks against an external retained manifest. Source-safe packaging excludes downloaded PDFs/OCR/HTML, transfer helpers and private service identifiers. Technical reconstruction proves determinism and internal identities, not the economic truth of unverified issuer statements. Independent review and fresh Library-download tests were completed before the package was frozen.\\n\"","\"heading_start\"","\"body_start\"","\"end\"","\"original_heading\"","29","56","1959","\"## 1. Executive assessment\\n\"","2003","4015","\"## 2. Research header, gates and confidence\\n\"","4059","7775","\"## 3. Canonical scorecard and scoring audit\\n\"","7817","9868","\"## 4. Business model and economic anatomy\\n\"","9920","12755","\"## 5. Competitive position and named economic peers\\n\"","12796","14388","\"## 6. Revenue quality and organic growth\\n\"","14439","18067","\"## 7. Growth, reinvestment and incremental capital\\n\"","18116","20388","\"## 8. Management, governance and execution audit\\n\"","20438","24672","\"## 9. Financial forensics and full audit sequence\\n\"","24737","34675","\"## 10. Valuation, cash-linked scenarios and reverse expectations\\n\"","34729","36082","\"## 11. Strongest bear case and thesis-break variables\\n\"","36130","36968","\"## 12. Five-times/ten-times and compounder test\\n\"","37009","38654","\"## 13. Monitoring and information needed\\n\"","38704","39891","\"## 14. Final classification and update discipline\\n\"","39953","43459","\"## 15. Sources, assumptions, recovery and final quality check\\n\"","43453","\"filename\"","\"format\"","\"content_sha256\"","\"historical_inputs.json\"","\"c3151aaab563f18a31f4dd0ce8b29c72ed7cf34f57bfd35a89df9e311b615